WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:08.260
- Hi, good evening.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:09.950
My name is Alan Gerber.

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:12.790
I'm the director of the
Institution for Social

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:14.330
and Policy Studies at Yale.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:18.960
ISPS is holding a series
of events this year,

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.850
bringing together experts
from Yale and beyond,

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:25.310
to shed light on key questions
concerning American politics,

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:27.610
governance and democracy.

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:31.520
Tonight's event is
sponsored by ISPS Democracy,

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:35.220
which is an ISP program that
aims to further research,

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.870
and public discussion,

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:40.450
of representation and
democratic policy making.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:43.260
We have a number of events
planned for this spring.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.100
Please be on the lookout for them.

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:49.450
If you would like to learn
more about these ISPS events,

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:53.520
through ISPS Democracy
and also ISPS Research,

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.630
please visit the ISPS
website, isps.yale.edu.

00:01:01.357 --> 00:01:02.810
And I would like to just begin

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:04.570
by not only thanking the panelists,

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:08.350
but also thanking the
organizers of the panel,

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:13.350
Pam Greene, Pam Lamonaca,
Tory Bilski and Limor Peer.

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:17.470
So, here's the logistics
for tonight's event.

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:19.970
After I introduce the panelists,

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:24.470
and we have a panel presentation
and a panel discussion,

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:27.120
there will be a question
and answer period.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.850
Please send questions
throughout the session,

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:33.860
using the Q and A button at
the bottom of the Zoom screen.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:37.670
It's very useful, if you
can keep your questions

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:42.670
to a relatively compact
short sentence or two,

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:47.420
that facilitates both our
ability to process the question,

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:51.410
and also to field it to the panelists.

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:55.430
I also wanna note that tonight's
event is being recorded,

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:57.900
and also that if you need
technical assistance,

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:01.300
just email isps@yale.edu.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:06.270
So, let me now welcome
tonight's panelists.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:10.470
I'm gonna introduce all three
of the panelists at once,

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:14.320
and then they're gonna
speak in alphabetical order,

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.150
once the introduction is concluded.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:22.650
First, Phil Goff is the
Carl Hovland Professor,

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:26.520
of African American Studies in Psychology.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:31.740
Phil joined the faculty in 2020.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:33.890
He is the co-founder and CEO

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:36.610
of the Center for Policing Equity,

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:39.620
which is the world's largest
research and action think tank,

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:41.570
on race and policing.

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:44.560
And it hosts an unparalleled collection,

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.800
of police behavioral data,

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.590
in the National Science Foundation funded,

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.540
National Justice Database.

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:55.780
Professor Goff is a
recognized national leader,

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:58.350
in the science of
policing and racial bias.

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:02.940
Dr. Goff has won two American
Psychological Association

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:04.560
Early Career Awards,

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.020
the Association for Psychological
Science Rising Star Award,

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:10.440
and the National Organization

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:14.120
of Black Law Enforcement
Executives Lloyd Sealy Award,

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:15.940
among many others.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:20.940
He was also a panelist on
President Obama's Task Force,

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.790
on 21st century policing has provided,

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:27.640
congressional testimony on many occasions

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:32.410
and regularly appears on cable
news and other media outlets.

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:37.240
We're delighted to have
Phil Goff here this evening.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:41.590
Our second speaker will be Jacob Hacker.

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:47.530
Jacob is the Stanley B Resor
Professor of Political Science,

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:51.980
and he served as ISPS
director from 2011 to 2020.

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:57.200
He's a member of the American
Academy of Arts and Sciences,

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:00.180
the National Academy of Social Insurance,

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:04.660
and also was the 2020
Robert Ball Award winner,

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:09.290
for the National Academy
of Social Insurance.

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:11.790
He's known for his writings and research,

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:14.620
on American politics and public policy,

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:18.060
and as a pioneer in the
development of healthcare policy

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:19.810
especially the public option.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:23.610
Look forward to your remarks, Jacob.

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:26.700
And then we have Fiona Scott Morton.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:31.350
Scott Morton is the Theodore
Nierenberg Professor

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:34.170
of Economics at the School of Management,

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:37.310
has been on the faculty since 1999,

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:42.310
served as SOM Associate
Dean from 2007 to 2010.

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:47.860
And from 2011 to 2012,
Professor Scott Morton,

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:50.880
served as the Deputy
Assistant Attorney General

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:53.790
for Economics at the Antitrust Division

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:56.280
of the US Department of Justice,

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:59.070
where she helped formulate and enforce,

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:02.300
the nation's antitrust laws and policies.

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:07.300
In 2019, Scott Morton founded
the Thurmond Arnold Project

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:10.370
at Yale named in honor of Thurmond Arnold,

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:13.570
Yale law professor and head
of the Antitrust Division,

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:16.200
from 1938 to 1943.

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:17.900
And this is a program that brings together

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:19.860
Yale faculty and students,

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:22.170
from across the
university, the law school,

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:23.940
the school of management,

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:27.390
as well as scholars from other
institutions to collaborate

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:31.650
on research related to
competition, competition policy,

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:33.700
and antitrust enforcement.

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:37.410
Welcome Fiona, I look forward
to your remarks as well.

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:40.210
So, with that, we can get started.

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:43.280
Phil, we'll start off
then Jacob then Fiona

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:46.670
- Thanks very much, Alan.

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:49.870
And thank you everybody
for spending part of this,

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:54.870
at least in the Northeast,
very snowy Tuesday in February.

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:57.500
My understanding is that
according to my students,

00:05:57.500 --> 00:05:59.910
every time I lecture it snows.

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:02.570
So, I was not aware that coming
to Yale gave me these swarms

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:04.890
of superpowers over nature,

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:07.380
but thank you also for that.

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:11.120
We were asked to talk about two things,

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:12.970
in a sort of brief set
of introductory remarks.

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:16.360
One is something that we
think is going to happen,

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:18.150
in our issue area.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:20.920
And one is something that
we think should happen,

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:22.680
and I assume it was implied,

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:24.820
but we're not as confident is going to.

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:27.150
So, to give you a context

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:29.390
of sort of what I
imagine my issue area is,

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:34.410
it's the body politic of
race in the United States,

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:36.400
but particularly within public safety.

00:06:37.526 --> 00:06:39.970
I don't know if any of you
guys noticed over the summer,

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:41.520
but that was in the news a lot.

00:06:42.721 --> 00:06:45.350
Part of the reason was
obviously the conflagration

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:46.790
that started in Minneapolis,

00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:48.500
swept across the entire country,

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:51.540
and then became a global enterprise.

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:55.520
Really a global reckoning
with issues of state violence,

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:58.480
race, and social hierarchy.

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:01.230
But part of it is part of the cycle,

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:03.100
that we go through in this country,

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:05.240
where we look at,

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:06.920
I won't say we reckon
with 'cause we don't,

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:11.070
but we look at the
massive past due notice,

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:14.790
of the unpaid debt owed to
black folks in this country.

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:18.510
I'm not necessarily only talking

00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:20.830
about reparations for slavery,

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:25.130
but I am also talking about
slavery and its consequences.

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:27.730
And in the wake of the summer,

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:31.000
part of what we saw was
an awakening to literally,

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:34.040
they're calling it
re-imagining public safety.

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:37.230
But it's an awakening of
the public imagination,

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:40.210
for what's possible in
response to violations

00:07:40.210 --> 00:07:43.330
of the social contract,
amongst the most vulnerable.

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:45.570
Think about it like this.

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:51.700
If I choose to take Alan's wallet, right,

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:53.910
then we can understand that,

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:56.720
as me choosing to
violate the sacred trust,

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:59.350
that allows us to live
together, that social contract,

00:07:59.350 --> 00:08:01.860
and I should have some consequences.

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:05.020
But if part of the calculus
for me taking Alan's wallet,

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:09.410
is Alan or other deans
having taken so much from me,

00:08:09.410 --> 00:08:11.900
that there aren't really
other good options, right?

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:14.110
I understand that this
metaphor gets fantastical,

00:08:14.110 --> 00:08:15.580
when we're talking about
senior faculty at Yale,

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:17.930
but run with me for a little bit here.

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:21.040
If the deal is that I have been placed,

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:23.800
with a band set of
options and I choose one,

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:25.430
that violates the social contract,

00:08:25.430 --> 00:08:28.710
when in reality, the
social contract really just

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:31.080
never included me in the first place.

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:34.870
Why is punishment our
only response to that?

00:08:36.070 --> 00:08:39.090
Why is it that when someone
chooses a bad option,

00:08:39.090 --> 00:08:41.680
with a set of bad
choices in front of them,

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:45.700
that they have that punishment
funded by the State,

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:47.850
in communities that are
disproportionately inhabited

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:50.160
by the descendants of
formerly enslaved people

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:53.400
in this country, why is
that our policy solution?

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:56.810
So, that's a big part of
what this new administration

00:08:56.810 --> 00:08:58.470
is having to wrestle with.

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:01.880
And in that moment, the
defund and the re-imagining,

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:04.600
another word has more recently appeared,

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:05.950
and that word is abolition.

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:09.700
And I wanna to couch
both of my answers about,

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:11.870
what I think will happen,
what I think should happen

00:09:11.870 --> 00:09:13.270
in the context of abolition.

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:16.120
The way we talk about abolition

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:17.390
of the prison industrial complex,

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:20.610
which is historically has
been mostly prisons and jails,

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:23.700
and now increasingly
includes sheriffs and police.

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:27.250
We think about that as simply
the removal of the bad thing,

00:09:27.250 --> 00:09:30.770
the cancer that is that is
poisoning the body politic.

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:34.720
But one of the greatest sort
of champions of the idea

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:37.580
of particularly prison
abolition, Angela Davis,

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:39.890
when she writes in 2005,
essentially a retrospective

00:09:39.890 --> 00:09:43.150
of her life's work to that point,

00:09:43.150 --> 00:09:47.320
she calls the book, "Abolition Democracy,"

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:52.210
which is a hearkening back
to the language of Du Bois

00:09:52.210 --> 00:09:55.130
in 1935, when he's talking
about black reconstruction.

00:09:55.130 --> 00:09:59.000
And her first book in 1971,
while incarcerated, right,

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:00.460
called, "When They Come in the Morning,"

00:10:00.460 --> 00:10:01.840
it's a collection of essays from people

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:04.960
who have been involved in
the criminal legal system,

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:07.720
and she chooses Julian Bond
to write the forward to that.

00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:09.220
And what does Julian Bond write about,

00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:13.090
Du Bois in 1935 saying, no
one has done anything since.

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:16.680
So, when Du Bois talks
about abolition democracy,

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:21.680
he's not talking about the
US system post emancipation,

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:23.390
post-slavery.

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:26.010
He's talking about an
affirmative construction,

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:29.080
of political and systemic structures,

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:32.640
that rent the body politic
of the toxins of slavery.

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:35.610
It's not just the removal of the injury,

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:36.850
it's the healing from it,

00:10:36.850 --> 00:10:39.920
that needs to take
generations afterwards, right?

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:43.500
Now, that word abolition is straining,

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:45.790
what's been possible on the political left

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:47.320
and activist basis.

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:50.730
'Cause it's set up as counter two reform.

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:52.700
The idea that you could
fix the thing that is,

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:56.100
no, sometimes you've got to
burn it down and start a new.

00:10:56.100 --> 00:11:00.290
That's the conversation happening
outside of Washington, DC,

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:03.570
in terms of how we manage things
that will gotta call crime

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:05.410
but really it's violations
of the social contract,

00:11:05.410 --> 00:11:07.280
among the most vulnerable.

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:09.490
So, as my time starts to
begin to come to an end,

00:11:09.490 --> 00:11:11.190
what do I think is going to happen?

00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:12.690
And what do I think should happen?

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:15.140
What's going to happen is that,

00:11:15.140 --> 00:11:17.260
this administration which
is made up of a mix,

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:20.430
of folks who are old
guard, people doing reform,

00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:22.470
trying to fix the things that we've got,

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:25.140
I have a mix of folks
who are really excited

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:26.830
for something new,

00:11:26.830 --> 00:11:29.350
there is going to be
stuff put on the table,

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:31.350
that looks exactly like what it did,

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:34.060
at the end of the previous administration.

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:37.360
That makes specifically the
Justice and Policing Act, right?

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:39.820
This is a bill that
passed out of the house,

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:42.120
didn't get a vote on
the floor of the Senate,

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:45.680
in part because there were
competing ideas about it.

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:49.430
But in part because, frankly
there was no incentive,

00:11:49.430 --> 00:11:51.150
for anybody to introduce it.

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:53.380
What it will do is set
for the bunch of things,

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:55.480
that will make policing better.

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:56.640
Reduce qualified immunity,

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:58.750
which means that if a
police officer did something

00:11:58.750 --> 00:12:01.180
that was unreasonable, they
could be held accountable to it.

00:12:01.180 --> 00:12:03.210
It'll lower the 242 standard,

00:12:03.210 --> 00:12:06.250
which means that instead of
a willful discrimination,

00:12:06.250 --> 00:12:07.820
you can get reckless.

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:10.220
It will allow the federal
government to engage in things

00:12:10.220 --> 00:12:12.090
around accountability and public safety,

00:12:12.090 --> 00:12:13.340
they haven't done before.

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:15.900
But what it won't do,

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:17.790
is set up the structures
that prevent someone

00:12:17.790 --> 00:12:20.560
from having to call
911 in the first place.

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:23.690
And it also won't allow for the resources,

00:12:23.690 --> 00:12:25.850
that are the appropriate response,

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:27.350
for instance, with mental health crisis

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:31.400
or substance abuse crisis
or crisis of suicide.

00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:33.300
It won't send those resources,

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:36.190
in response and it won't
send them affirmatively.

00:12:36.190 --> 00:12:39.260
So, the kind of legislation
that can get through Congress

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:40.300
and the kind of executive orders

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:44.090
that will come out of
the president's desk,

00:12:44.090 --> 00:12:45.280
the first time through,

00:12:46.130 --> 00:12:47.480
that's gonna be the reform.

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:48.910
The things that are bold action,

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:51.970
in the context of what
happened before this summer.

00:12:53.270 --> 00:12:56.900
But what should happen, and
I'm less sanguine about,

00:12:57.850 --> 00:13:01.000
is a fool reckoning of
what our systems of justice

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:04.350
at the federal level
have failed to render,

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:08.050
particularly to black,
brown and native folks,

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:09.600
in this country.

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:13.080
And I mean, quite literally,
a reckoning and accounting,

00:13:14.080 --> 00:13:19.080
what has DOJ done that
has harmed native folks,

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:22.050
Chicano folks, black folks?

00:13:22.970 --> 00:13:24.700
Where can we get that codified?

00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:28.100
'Cause that reckoning is the first step,

00:13:28.100 --> 00:13:30.040
towards actually fixing.

00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:33.090
I'm not calling for
massive amounts of funding

00:13:33.090 --> 00:13:34.790
for all of that yet.

00:13:34.790 --> 00:13:36.970
I'm calling for the infrastructure,

00:13:36.970 --> 00:13:39.060
that makes it morally irresistible,

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:40.840
to take that next step.

00:13:40.840 --> 00:13:43.690
Because as we're moving towards
progress along public safety

00:13:43.690 --> 00:13:46.040
where I'm very concerned,

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:47.740
there will be a critical incident.

00:13:49.020 --> 00:13:52.390
There will be a murder,
there'll be an assault,

00:13:52.390 --> 00:13:54.690
there'll be something ghastly.

00:13:54.690 --> 00:13:57.750
There'll be those who seek
to weaponize that incident,

00:13:58.670 --> 00:14:02.960
as a cause to reinvest in the
kind of policing we've had,

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:05.220
up until now.

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:09.460
To humiliate or to embarrass
or to shame the folks,

00:14:09.460 --> 00:14:12.010
who've been calling for
more radical change.

00:14:13.010 --> 00:14:15.650
And if the infrastructure isn't set up,

00:14:15.650 --> 00:14:17.300
so that we can continue to move forward,

00:14:17.300 --> 00:14:19.840
when it's some of these
experiments that we're trying out

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:22.210
in many of the cities
across the country fail,

00:14:23.220 --> 00:14:24.970
then we're gonna do the
exact same thing we've done,

00:14:24.970 --> 00:14:26.090
every other time that we've tried

00:14:26.090 --> 00:14:27.730
to reimagine public safety,

00:14:27.730 --> 00:14:31.230
which is forget about the
people who most need that safety

00:14:31.230 --> 00:14:35.470
and retrench ourselves in old orthodoxies.

00:14:35.470 --> 00:14:37.730
So, my hope for this administration,

00:14:37.730 --> 00:14:41.590
is that there's enough new
energy, both inside and outside,

00:14:41.590 --> 00:14:43.510
that the pressure stays on,

00:14:43.510 --> 00:14:47.380
that we build for the long-term
and not just in reaction

00:14:47.380 --> 00:14:49.860
to the passions of this summer.

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:51.740
But what I know is going to happen,

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:55.280
is that incremental change
is going to move forward.

00:14:55.280 --> 00:14:56.490
And it's going to be up to us,

00:14:56.490 --> 00:14:58.540
to decide whether or not that's enough.

00:15:03.980 --> 00:15:05.160
- Well, first of all,

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:07.820
thank you so much everyone for being here.

00:15:07.820 --> 00:15:11.930
Thank you, Alan, Lamar, Pam,
for putting this together.

00:15:11.930 --> 00:15:16.060
Thanks Fiona and Phil for
being part of this panel,

00:15:16.060 --> 00:15:17.170
and thank Phil in particular

00:15:17.170 --> 00:15:18.870
for those really stirring remarks.

00:15:20.420 --> 00:15:24.200
My conclusions, my issue
area is completely different,

00:15:24.200 --> 00:15:26.140
but my conclusions are somewhat similar,

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:28.840
and maybe this is going to
be a theme for the evening,

00:15:29.930 --> 00:15:32.200
because the gap between
what should be done,

00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:35.370
and what will be possible
in the immediate term,

00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:36.500
is pretty great.

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:39.180
Not withstanding the
fact that those who want

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:41.370
to act boldly in these areas,

00:15:41.370 --> 00:15:44.320
are in a stronger position
than we would have expected

00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:45.350
a year or two ago.

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:49.720
So, let me say a bit about the issue area,

00:15:49.720 --> 00:15:51.230
it's not gonna be a surprise to you,

00:15:51.230 --> 00:15:53.260
that I'm gonna talk a
bit about healthcare.

00:15:53.260 --> 00:15:55.100
Alan already said that, but I wanna,

00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:58.960
that's a huge area and I'm not
an expert on public health.

00:15:58.960 --> 00:16:01.290
So, I'm not going to say a huge amount

00:16:01.290 --> 00:16:04.990
or very much at all about
the response to COVID 19.

00:16:06.083 --> 00:16:09.290
I'm gonna focus mostly on
the unfinished business

00:16:09.290 --> 00:16:12.070
of American Healthcare Reform.

00:16:12.070 --> 00:16:16.560
Namely the fact that the
United States is the only,

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:20.630
rich democracy that lacks the
kind of two part solution,

00:16:20.630 --> 00:16:24.390
that every other country in
that set has come up with,

00:16:24.390 --> 00:16:25.950
a system that covers everyone,

00:16:25.950 --> 00:16:30.950
and has some mechanism for
controlling costs system-wide.

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:33.700
And in particular, for
restraining healthcare prices,

00:16:33.700 --> 00:16:38.020
which in the absence of such systems,

00:16:38.020 --> 00:16:41.950
tend to grow much more quickly
than the cost of living,

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:45.940
than people's standard,
than people's incomes,

00:16:45.940 --> 00:16:47.790
and than what governments can afford.

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:52.110
Fiona knows a lot about
that side of the story,

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:54.350
and I'll be interested
to see if her thoughts

00:16:54.350 --> 00:16:57.980
on anti-trust extended at all
into the healthcare realm,

00:16:57.980 --> 00:17:00.460
but the areas I'm gonna focus on really,

00:17:00.460 --> 00:17:02.620
are around the broadening
of health insurance,

00:17:02.620 --> 00:17:05.870
and whether or not that
broadening can go hand in hand,

00:17:05.870 --> 00:17:07.270
with serious efforts to reign

00:17:07.270 --> 00:17:10.040
in America's exorbitant healthcare costs.

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:14.520
Now, as I said, my conclusions
are somewhat similar

00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:16.770
to Phil's, right?

00:17:16.770 --> 00:17:18.190
We need to build for the long-term,

00:17:18.190 --> 00:17:21.010
because we're not gonna see the kind of,

00:17:21.010 --> 00:17:24.360
transformative change we
need in the near term.

00:17:24.360 --> 00:17:25.940
But that's not to say,

00:17:25.940 --> 00:17:28.560
that the changes in the
near term could not,

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:30.810
both make an enormous difference,

00:17:30.810 --> 00:17:34.650
and contribute to that
longterm transformation.

00:17:34.650 --> 00:17:37.770
Now, what is the should,

00:17:37.770 --> 00:17:40.350
what are the transformative
changes we need?

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:43.927
Now, I mean, I think not
withstanding the fact

00:17:43.927 --> 00:17:48.927
that we have, we're very far away,

00:17:49.510 --> 00:17:52.140
from having universal insurance

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:55.150
and effective cost control today.

00:17:55.150 --> 00:17:57.700
I actually think a lot
of the core elements

00:17:57.700 --> 00:18:00.930
of what needs to be done, have
actually been articulated,

00:18:00.930 --> 00:18:02.700
and not just by movement leaders

00:18:02.700 --> 00:18:04.610
but by people in public office.

00:18:04.610 --> 00:18:09.000
And I won't try to make
the argument for why,

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:13.130
Medicare for all may be a bridge
too far in the medium term,

00:18:13.130 --> 00:18:15.430
but instead just say that,

00:18:15.430 --> 00:18:18.720
whatever your views on
whether we can move,

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:21.810
to a universal public insurance system,

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:23.960
there is broad agreement

00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:28.540
that we need to massively
expand public coverage,

00:18:28.540 --> 00:18:33.540
and the cost controls and
rationalization that goes with it,

00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:35.860
that we need to make sure that,

00:18:35.860 --> 00:18:39.570
every citizen and
eventually every resident

00:18:39.570 --> 00:18:40.820
in the United States,

00:18:40.820 --> 00:18:43.550
has protection against
ruinous healthcare costs,

00:18:43.550 --> 00:18:45.610
and access to medical care.

00:18:45.610 --> 00:18:50.610
And notably, that we need to do this,

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:53.130
with an understanding
of what we've learned

00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:54.380
over the last generation,

00:18:54.380 --> 00:18:59.380
namely that the cost of
healthcare is growing so quickly,

00:19:01.187 --> 00:19:06.187
and the private insurance models,

00:19:06.530 --> 00:19:10.060
are shifting so much
more costs onto patients,

00:19:10.060 --> 00:19:12.580
and restricting networks such that,

00:19:12.580 --> 00:19:14.570
unless we put in place a system

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:18.580
that allows people to have
broad access to providers,

00:19:20.190 --> 00:19:24.190
relatively affordable out
of pocket costs for care,

00:19:24.190 --> 00:19:29.190
and coverage that's
there when they need it,

00:19:29.750 --> 00:19:31.030
we're not gonna achieve that.

00:19:31.030 --> 00:19:33.110
So, it's gonna require public actions.

00:19:34.330 --> 00:19:39.330
Before the election, the
Biden campaign actually,

00:19:40.630 --> 00:19:45.630
articulated in concert
with the Sanders Camp,

00:19:46.390 --> 00:19:48.630
a set of principles that are now known

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:51.790
as the unity task force document.

00:19:51.790 --> 00:19:54.620
And the important aspect of that was that,

00:19:54.620 --> 00:19:56.000
a core thing that they said

00:19:56.000 --> 00:19:58.790
was that they wanted to have
a public insurance option,

00:19:58.790 --> 00:20:03.300
such as the one that I've
advocated for many years.

00:20:03.300 --> 00:20:05.900
And that would be built on
the Medicare infrastructure,

00:20:05.900 --> 00:20:10.900
and would use Medicare providers
and Medicare payment rates,

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:12.860
but improve those over time.

00:20:12.860 --> 00:20:14.810
And that it would be
used to expand coverage

00:20:14.810 --> 00:20:16.760
for people who live in States,

00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:19.390
that where Medicaid was not expanded

00:20:19.390 --> 00:20:20.800
in response to Affordable Care Act.

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:24.340
And I think if it were possible,

00:20:24.340 --> 00:20:27.340
that would be the right
template to pursue.

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:30.570
The challenge of course, is that,

00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:33.670
we are despite the fact that
those who are advocating

00:20:33.670 --> 00:20:36.780
these reforms are in a majority position,

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:40.410
in the sense that the Senate,
the House and the White House,

00:20:40.410 --> 00:20:41.700
are in the hands of the party

00:20:41.700 --> 00:20:43.800
that would like to do these things,

00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:46.270
that party has a very narrow majority.

00:20:46.270 --> 00:20:49.270
And so, we've all been
debating and discussing,

00:20:49.270 --> 00:20:51.320
the filibuster and its future,

00:20:51.320 --> 00:20:54.680
and to a very great extent,
the extent of changes

00:20:54.680 --> 00:20:56.470
that are possible are gonna depend,

00:20:56.470 --> 00:20:59.700
on whether or not the filibuster
is retained in the Senate.

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:05.290
That debate will not play out
in the context of healthcare,

00:21:05.290 --> 00:21:08.910
it will likely play out in
the context of voting reform.

00:21:08.910 --> 00:21:12.760
And I don't know how it
will be resolved though.

00:21:12.760 --> 00:21:17.760
I must say that there is more
prospect for the elimination,

00:21:17.940 --> 00:21:19.540
of the filibuster for
ordinary legislation,

00:21:19.540 --> 00:21:23.900
and there has been at any
point in the last 25 years,

00:21:23.900 --> 00:21:28.060
but that that prospect
still seems fairly elusive,

00:21:28.060 --> 00:21:30.590
given the current configuration of power.

00:21:30.590 --> 00:21:32.590
So, given that I've written recently

00:21:32.590 --> 00:21:34.870
that I think it's very important that,

00:21:34.870 --> 00:21:38.580
those who are in support of
the more ambitious reforms

00:21:38.580 --> 00:21:39.600
I've articulated,

00:21:39.600 --> 00:21:41.680
or even more ambitious reforms,

00:21:41.680 --> 00:21:45.940
like moving to a
universal Medicare system,

00:21:45.940 --> 00:21:48.130
really need to think about how to use,

00:21:48.130 --> 00:21:52.330
the opportunities that we
have in the medium term,

00:21:52.330 --> 00:21:54.410
to lay the groundwork
for longer-term change.

00:21:54.410 --> 00:21:56.290
So, this is really
similar to the challenge,

00:21:56.290 --> 00:21:57.900
that Phil was talking about.

00:21:57.900 --> 00:21:59.910
I do think it's about movement building,

00:21:59.910 --> 00:22:02.570
but it's also about
doing what I've called,

00:22:02.570 --> 00:22:05.900
using power to build, using
policy to build power.

00:22:05.900 --> 00:22:07.890
So, how do you put in place policies,

00:22:07.890 --> 00:22:10.350
that will build constituencies and trust

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:13.400
and positive results that
will make it more likely

00:22:13.400 --> 00:22:15.700
that you can move towards
more ambitious reforms

00:22:15.700 --> 00:22:16.820
in the future?

00:22:16.820 --> 00:22:18.230
I've wrote a whole article on this,

00:22:18.230 --> 00:22:20.998
in the journal of health,
politics, policy and law,

00:22:20.998 --> 00:22:22.150
that just came out.

00:22:22.150 --> 00:22:25.620
But the three elements
that I've emphasized are,

00:22:25.620 --> 00:22:30.620
those who are seeking these
more ambitious reforms,

00:22:30.820 --> 00:22:32.830
need to upgrade the Affordable Care Act

00:22:32.830 --> 00:22:34.910
and in particular, to make it more,

00:22:34.910 --> 00:22:38.060
it's subsidies more generous for coverage,

00:22:38.060 --> 00:22:41.910
and to fix a lot of the defects in the law

00:22:41.910 --> 00:22:46.340
that had been introduced
or were not fixed,

00:22:46.340 --> 00:22:49.940
in the last administration.

00:22:49.940 --> 00:22:52.130
And there's already a
lot of executive action,

00:22:52.130 --> 00:22:53.110
that's moved in that direction,

00:22:53.110 --> 00:22:55.650
but it's gonna take a legislation.

00:22:55.650 --> 00:22:57.270
The second thing that I've argued is that,

00:22:57.270 --> 00:23:00.640
you need to build the
infrastructure for bigger reforms,

00:23:00.640 --> 00:23:04.180
and that would mean improving
and expanding key aspects,

00:23:04.180 --> 00:23:06.980
of Medicare in particular,

00:23:06.980 --> 00:23:10.010
making Medicare a better
program for older Americans

00:23:10.010 --> 00:23:11.880
and a more efficient program.

00:23:11.880 --> 00:23:14.080
And then finally,

00:23:14.080 --> 00:23:17.570
I think it's extremely important
to try to build movements

00:23:17.570 --> 00:23:18.940
and that's been involved policy.

00:23:18.940 --> 00:23:21.270
So, if you think about the COVID response,

00:23:21.270 --> 00:23:23.320
the idea of building a
public health jobs core

00:23:23.320 --> 00:23:25.470
that could help enroll
people and coverage,

00:23:25.470 --> 00:23:27.680
could be a really important part of also,

00:23:28.550 --> 00:23:32.760
building political power
and grassroots enthusiasm,

00:23:32.760 --> 00:23:34.980
for broader reforms in the future.

00:23:34.980 --> 00:23:37.110
The last thing I'll say is that,

00:23:37.110 --> 00:23:39.510
there really is in the
absence of the filibuster

00:23:39.510 --> 00:23:42.240
only one fast track in
American politics today,

00:23:42.240 --> 00:23:45.000
it's called the budget
reconciliation process.

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:49.260
And the budget reconciliation
process will be the vehicle

00:23:49.260 --> 00:23:52.260
for the current legislation,

00:23:52.260 --> 00:23:55.260
the pandemic relief
legislation before Congress.

00:23:55.260 --> 00:23:58.130
You only get one and at most in this year,

00:23:58.130 --> 00:24:01.600
two bites of the reconciliation apple.

00:24:01.600 --> 00:24:04.330
And that means that the
challenge is gonna be,

00:24:04.330 --> 00:24:07.560
whether the next big
reconciliation bill will,

00:24:07.560 --> 00:24:10.660
include core healthcare provisions.

00:24:10.660 --> 00:24:14.570
Remember there's also a huge
push for infrastructure,

00:24:14.570 --> 00:24:17.800
and a abroad response to climate change.

00:24:17.800 --> 00:24:19.820
And I think that there's a real question,

00:24:19.820 --> 00:24:22.220
of whether or not you can do these kinds

00:24:22.220 --> 00:24:24.490
of reforms simultaneously.

00:24:24.490 --> 00:24:27.090
And next year, we're already going to be

00:24:27.090 --> 00:24:28.650
into an election year that's gonna be,

00:24:28.650 --> 00:24:30.970
deeply freighted in 2022.

00:24:30.970 --> 00:24:35.480
And so, to me in the absence
of filibuster reform,

00:24:35.480 --> 00:24:38.080
the agenda for healthcare reform,

00:24:38.080 --> 00:24:39.840
is likely gonna be more limited

00:24:39.840 --> 00:24:41.710
than many advocates would want,

00:24:41.710 --> 00:24:44.650
simply because of the
crowded reform agenda,

00:24:44.650 --> 00:24:47.840
that's gonna occur with
only one big fast train,

00:24:47.840 --> 00:24:49.130
leaving the station.

00:24:49.130 --> 00:24:51.670
But with that, I'm gonna
return to my earlier remarks

00:24:51.670 --> 00:24:54.206
and say, that one should not despair,

00:24:54.206 --> 00:24:57.320
but think about the ways in
which these opportunities

00:24:57.320 --> 00:24:59.310
in the near term could be used to build,

00:24:59.310 --> 00:25:01.450
power and better policy for the longterm.

00:25:04.460 --> 00:25:06.640
- Great, I guess I'm up next.

00:25:06.640 --> 00:25:09.170
It was wonderful to listen to both of you.

00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:12.650
I will actually echo Jacob and say,

00:25:12.650 --> 00:25:16.110
that I have that same theme
in the following sense.

00:25:16.110 --> 00:25:18.640
My area of expertise is, as Alan said,

00:25:18.640 --> 00:25:20.600
competition policy in antitrust.

00:25:21.720 --> 00:25:24.410
And people don't usually
think of antitrust

00:25:24.410 --> 00:25:26.370
as social justice.

00:25:26.370 --> 00:25:29.260
But it is for the following reason.

00:25:29.260 --> 00:25:30.730
Most shareholders,

00:25:30.730 --> 00:25:35.730
most people entitled to corporate
profits, monopoly profit,

00:25:36.540 --> 00:25:41.410
are the top 1%, and the
top 1% are mostly white.

00:25:41.410 --> 00:25:44.100
And when you take a dollar
of corporate profit,

00:25:44.100 --> 00:25:47.447
and you turn it into competition instead,

00:25:47.447 --> 00:25:49.610
those monopoly profits don't get earned,

00:25:49.610 --> 00:25:52.890
and they get turned into low
prices or better quality.

00:25:52.890 --> 00:25:54.800
And those benefit all consumers.

00:25:54.800 --> 00:25:57.270
And all consumers are
obviously much more diverse

00:25:57.270 --> 00:25:59.980
than the top one or 5%.

00:25:59.980 --> 00:26:02.530
So, when we enforce the competition,

00:26:02.530 --> 00:26:04.620
then when we make our
markets more competitive,

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:07.710
we are in fact redistributing income,

00:26:07.710 --> 00:26:09.130
reducing income inequality,

00:26:09.130 --> 00:26:11.970
and helping the more
vulnerable disproportionately.

00:26:13.820 --> 00:26:16.680
So, the last 40 years of evidence,

00:26:16.680 --> 00:26:19.690
has now been made much more clear,

00:26:19.690 --> 00:26:22.030
I would say over the
last five or 10 years,

00:26:22.030 --> 00:26:23.720
to tell us that we have
competition problem

00:26:23.720 --> 00:26:24.610
in the United States,

00:26:24.610 --> 00:26:26.390
we do not have enough competitive markets,

00:26:26.390 --> 00:26:29.680
we have too many monopolies
and settings with market power.

00:26:30.730 --> 00:26:32.190
What should we do about that?

00:26:33.790 --> 00:26:37.270
One thing we could do is to
strengthen the antitrust laws.

00:26:37.270 --> 00:26:38.950
This is already happening.

00:26:38.950 --> 00:26:41.740
Senator Amy Klobuchar has
already introduced a bill,

00:26:41.740 --> 00:26:43.220
to strengthen the antitrust laws.

00:26:43.220 --> 00:26:46.050
This is excellent, there'll be more ideas,

00:26:46.050 --> 00:26:49.590
I think in this area, maybe
more bills that would be great.

00:26:49.590 --> 00:26:51.350
There's bipartisan support

00:26:51.350 --> 00:26:53.650
for reforming the antitrust
laws in the house.

00:26:55.040 --> 00:26:58.400
I expect that we will get a movement here,

00:26:58.400 --> 00:27:00.260
and the reason is because,

00:27:01.450 --> 00:27:03.360
additional antitrust enforcement

00:27:03.360 --> 00:27:04.770
and strong grantee trust laws,

00:27:04.770 --> 00:27:06.030
would generate more competition

00:27:06.030 --> 00:27:07.750
which as I said is good for consumers

00:27:07.750 --> 00:27:10.810
especially the bottom end,
that appeals to Democrats.

00:27:10.810 --> 00:27:13.060
Republicans in general
don't care about consumers,

00:27:13.060 --> 00:27:14.400
they care about corporate profit.

00:27:14.400 --> 00:27:16.930
And so, Republicans in
general have not been in favor

00:27:16.930 --> 00:27:18.220
of strong grantee trust laws,

00:27:18.220 --> 00:27:19.750
but rather weak grantee trust laws.

00:27:19.750 --> 00:27:22.850
And that's in fact why
we are where we are,

00:27:22.850 --> 00:27:26.430
but Republicans do value
small business, okay.

00:27:26.430 --> 00:27:28.770
And when you look at
corporate profits in America,

00:27:28.770 --> 00:27:31.020
what you see is a whitening,

00:27:31.020 --> 00:27:33.890
it's like income inequality
only for corporations.

00:27:33.890 --> 00:27:35.500
The money is all going to the biggest

00:27:35.500 --> 00:27:37.970
four or five platforms really.

00:27:37.970 --> 00:27:40.820
If you think about the ones
that everybody talks about,

00:27:40.820 --> 00:27:42.250
Google, Facebook Apple, Amazon,

00:27:42.250 --> 00:27:44.120
that's where all the money is.

00:27:44.120 --> 00:27:47.650
So, the small business
coalition runs from,

00:27:48.550 --> 00:27:50.400
AT and T and Comcast,

00:27:50.400 --> 00:27:53.630
and John Deere all the way
down to the corner coffee shop.

00:27:53.630 --> 00:27:56.120
All of those parties would like to,

00:27:56.120 --> 00:27:57.700
preserve their own profits,

00:27:57.700 --> 00:27:59.830
and not give them to Google.

00:27:59.830 --> 00:28:02.860
And so, they are willing
to ally with the consumers,

00:28:02.860 --> 00:28:04.640
to have stronger antitrust laws,

00:28:04.640 --> 00:28:08.730
and those corporations have
senators and representatives.

00:28:08.730 --> 00:28:10.680
So, it is possible I think,

00:28:10.680 --> 00:28:14.650
that we will get a antitrust reform soon.

00:28:14.650 --> 00:28:17.000
And what would that
antitrust reform look like?

00:28:17.880 --> 00:28:20.850
It would change the burden
of proof in more settings.

00:28:20.850 --> 00:28:24.260
That is to say, if two
corporations want to merge,

00:28:24.260 --> 00:28:26.330
rather than the government
needing to prove

00:28:26.330 --> 00:28:27.680
that that's harmful,

00:28:27.680 --> 00:28:31.210
we would make the corporations
prove that it's fine, okay.

00:28:31.210 --> 00:28:33.910
And what that does is
it just lowers the cost,

00:28:33.910 --> 00:28:36.530
to enforce the law because
the government can,

00:28:36.530 --> 00:28:37.780
doesn't have to spend as much money

00:28:37.780 --> 00:28:41.220
or time or resources to
figure out what's best to do.

00:28:41.220 --> 00:28:43.750
It increases the budgets of the agencies.

00:28:45.100 --> 00:28:47.730
The draft laws would repudiate,

00:28:47.730 --> 00:28:51.300
explicitly repudiate cases that
courts got wrong in the past

00:28:51.300 --> 00:28:53.480
where that allowed market power to build

00:28:53.480 --> 00:28:55.490
that protected monopolies,

00:28:55.490 --> 00:28:59.240
and a big area of interest
for economists and lawyers,

00:28:59.240 --> 00:29:03.650
both in this space is
protecting competition

00:29:03.650 --> 00:29:07.270
when nascent or potential competitors

00:29:07.270 --> 00:29:08.920
are excluded or bought.

00:29:08.920 --> 00:29:11.040
So, think about Facebook and Instagram,

00:29:11.040 --> 00:29:14.920
or any setting where the
rival isn't yet really fully

00:29:14.920 --> 00:29:16.930
in the market, but is coming,

00:29:16.930 --> 00:29:19.620
and the action of the
dominant firm is to buy them,

00:29:19.620 --> 00:29:20.640
or to exclude them.

00:29:20.640 --> 00:29:22.190
We want to try to protect competition

00:29:22.190 --> 00:29:25.000
in those cases where
oftentimes the benefit

00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:27.180
to consumers is innovation.

00:29:27.180 --> 00:29:28.720
It's not so much price,

00:29:28.720 --> 00:29:31.230
but it's about a new competitor entering

00:29:31.230 --> 00:29:33.540
and doing something in a different way.

00:29:33.540 --> 00:29:35.780
So, that's what I expect to happen.

00:29:35.780 --> 00:29:40.100
What should happen, is an
additional step beyond antitrust.

00:29:40.100 --> 00:29:41.850
So, if you think what happened

00:29:41.850 --> 00:29:45.530
between October and December of 2020,

00:29:45.530 --> 00:29:48.460
there were five or six
enormous antitrust cases

00:29:48.460 --> 00:29:50.740
that were filed by government plaintiffs.

00:29:50.740 --> 00:29:52.710
We had the house report
on digital platforms

00:29:52.710 --> 00:29:54.410
early in the fall as I mentioned,

00:29:54.410 --> 00:29:55.990
then the Department of Justice

00:29:55.990 --> 00:29:58.940
and some States sued Google
over it search monopoly,

00:29:58.940 --> 00:30:02.060
Texas and some other States
sued Google over ad tech,

00:30:02.060 --> 00:30:05.250
the FTC and some States sued Facebook,

00:30:05.250 --> 00:30:07.050
for its monopoly position.

00:30:07.050 --> 00:30:08.830
So, that was all very exciting,

00:30:08.830 --> 00:30:11.810
we caught up to Europe
after 10 years of delay,

00:30:11.810 --> 00:30:14.320
and everybody said, wow, we've done it.

00:30:14.320 --> 00:30:17.580
Now, the problem is that
once we come out of COVID,

00:30:17.580 --> 00:30:18.490
and insurrection,

00:30:18.490 --> 00:30:20.590
then we start to pay
attention to this again,

00:30:20.590 --> 00:30:23.330
we're gonna notice that the
first hearing in these case,

00:30:23.330 --> 00:30:26.700
is September 2023, okay.

00:30:26.700 --> 00:30:28.570
That we're gonna have the case,

00:30:28.570 --> 00:30:29.660
then we're gonna have to wait

00:30:29.660 --> 00:30:31.010
for the judge to write an opinion,

00:30:31.010 --> 00:30:32.780
then there'll be an
appeal and we have to wait

00:30:32.780 --> 00:30:34.650
for that to be scheduled
and we have to brief it.

00:30:34.650 --> 00:30:36.930
And then we have a
remedy stage at the end,

00:30:36.930 --> 00:30:40.400
and all in all this is
gonna take a long time.

00:30:40.400 --> 00:30:42.610
And I think citizens and the affirmation

00:30:42.610 --> 00:30:43.990
mentioned small businesses,

00:30:43.990 --> 00:30:45.780
are not gonna wanna wait that long,

00:30:45.780 --> 00:30:48.100
and they're gonna want to regulate.

00:30:48.100 --> 00:30:51.450
Europe in December 15th,

00:30:51.450 --> 00:30:55.170
released draft regulations
to control digital platforms,

00:30:55.170 --> 00:30:57.810
once again, lapping the United States,

00:30:58.740 --> 00:31:01.280
but we can watch those and react to them,

00:31:01.280 --> 00:31:04.110
and I think it's not
gonna take us 10 years,

00:31:04.110 --> 00:31:06.570
to pass some digital platform regulation.

00:31:06.570 --> 00:31:08.610
Again, the same coalition, the consumers,

00:31:08.610 --> 00:31:10.260
and small businesses,

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:14.310
and the things that we ought
to put in that regulation are,

00:31:15.350 --> 00:31:17.370
much, much more consumer protection,

00:31:17.370 --> 00:31:19.240
which we really don't have online.

00:31:19.240 --> 00:31:22.520
So, what kinds of
buttons can be pre-ticked

00:31:22.520 --> 00:31:23.820
when you go on a site?

00:31:23.820 --> 00:31:26.360
Buttons that make you buy
things and subscribe to things,

00:31:26.360 --> 00:31:28.380
and share your data, which
you didn't plan to do,

00:31:28.380 --> 00:31:30.070
but the buttons are pre-ticked.

00:31:30.070 --> 00:31:31.920
What about auto renewals?

00:31:31.920 --> 00:31:34.800
What about terms and conditions
that are 50 pages long,

00:31:34.800 --> 00:31:36.650
and just legally written the end,

00:31:36.650 --> 00:31:37.780
nobody understands them,

00:31:37.780 --> 00:31:39.970
and it could say give away
your first born in there

00:31:39.970 --> 00:31:42.630
and you would still click, I agree.

00:31:42.630 --> 00:31:45.860
What about uses for my data
that I don't fully understand

00:31:45.860 --> 00:31:49.230
and that are vague and maybe
actually aren't even adhered to

00:31:49.230 --> 00:31:52.670
by the platform that's
claiming they do that.

00:31:52.670 --> 00:31:54.680
There's other issues like interoperability

00:31:54.680 --> 00:31:55.590
between businesses.

00:31:55.590 --> 00:31:58.300
So, for example, when I send email

00:31:58.300 --> 00:32:03.300
from the Yale ISP to the, to
somebody on Gmail or Hotmail,

00:32:03.880 --> 00:32:05.870
or at Harvard, it arrives.

00:32:06.730 --> 00:32:10.020
And that is because
email is interoperable.

00:32:10.020 --> 00:32:12.370
Well, there are lots of digital platforms

00:32:12.370 --> 00:32:13.900
that are not interoperable,

00:32:13.900 --> 00:32:15.080
and that is a problem,

00:32:15.080 --> 00:32:18.680
because then I'm stuck
inside that platform,

00:32:18.680 --> 00:32:20.210
and I can't communicate outside of it.

00:32:20.210 --> 00:32:22.980
And that gives the platform
a lot of market power.

00:32:22.980 --> 00:32:25.330
So, interoperability would tremendously

00:32:25.330 --> 00:32:26.640
increase competition.

00:32:28.000 --> 00:32:29.450
And then the third thing that should go,

00:32:29.450 --> 00:32:30.320
I mean, there's lots of things

00:32:30.320 --> 00:32:31.400
you could put in this regulation,

00:32:31.400 --> 00:32:33.040
but I'm just gonna highlight
three of them for you.

00:32:33.040 --> 00:32:35.950
A third one I think is
really important is,

00:32:35.950 --> 00:32:39.360
thinking about the split of the
surplus between the platform

00:32:39.360 --> 00:32:43.090
and the everybody else, the
small businesses and the users.

00:32:43.090 --> 00:32:45.500
And that goes to some extent
to the bargaining power

00:32:45.500 --> 00:32:47.340
of those users and small businesses,

00:32:47.340 --> 00:32:48.860
because they don't have
anywhere else to go,

00:32:48.860 --> 00:32:49.900
because it's a monopoly.

00:32:49.900 --> 00:32:53.210
So, that's one way that this arises,

00:32:53.210 --> 00:32:57.300
but the other way is maybe
we should give businesses,

00:32:57.300 --> 00:32:59.700
and people property
rights over their data,

00:32:59.700 --> 00:33:03.020
so, that they can extract
something from the platform,

00:33:03.020 --> 00:33:04.380
for sharing it.

00:33:04.380 --> 00:33:07.270
Or the platform doesn't
get to use it for free,

00:33:07.270 --> 00:33:10.020
but has to actually talk to them about,

00:33:10.020 --> 00:33:13.300
whether they, what they
would like to be paid,

00:33:13.300 --> 00:33:14.600
for that data.

00:33:14.600 --> 00:33:19.000
Or for example, should
businesses be allowed to use,

00:33:19.000 --> 00:33:22.230
the features of the operating
system or the platform,

00:33:22.230 --> 00:33:25.230
that the platforms own applications use?

00:33:25.230 --> 00:33:27.830
So, the platforms have
access to certain APIs

00:33:27.830 --> 00:33:29.370
or functionality,

00:33:29.370 --> 00:33:31.890
should the competing
applications on the platform,

00:33:31.890 --> 00:33:35.110
also have access to those
APIs or functionality,

00:33:35.110 --> 00:33:38.220
that would be something
else regulation could do.

00:33:38.220 --> 00:33:40.780
So, those kinds of things
go into regulation.

00:33:40.780 --> 00:33:43.210
It's a sort of a pro
competitive, pro consumer,

00:33:43.210 --> 00:33:44.740
type of thing I have in mind.

00:33:44.740 --> 00:33:47.160
And I think, again,

00:33:47.160 --> 00:33:49.970
there's a broad coalition
that would like this.

00:33:49.970 --> 00:33:51.540
You need legislation to pass that,

00:33:51.540 --> 00:33:54.290
and you also probably need a new agency.

00:33:54.290 --> 00:33:56.730
A regulator that is designed to look

00:33:56.730 --> 00:33:58.190
after digital platforms,

00:33:58.190 --> 00:34:00.770
and that has a consumer
protection element,

00:34:00.770 --> 00:34:02.520
a competition element,

00:34:02.520 --> 00:34:06.670
and some special expertise
in digital businesses.

00:34:06.670 --> 00:34:09.870
Which by the way, will
be nearly everything,

00:34:09.870 --> 00:34:12.480
when you think about the
fact that stop and shop,

00:34:12.480 --> 00:34:14.660
is delivering me groceries,

00:34:14.660 --> 00:34:17.570
by letting me order over the internet.

00:34:17.570 --> 00:34:22.320
So, this is a very big remit
and it needs to be done well

00:34:22.320 --> 00:34:24.670
but I think it is important
to move forward on.

00:34:29.210 --> 00:34:30.680
- Okay, thank you all very much.

00:34:30.680 --> 00:34:34.270
That was really interesting
and great perspectives,

00:34:34.270 --> 00:34:38.830
on three really important
areas of public policy.

00:34:40.150 --> 00:34:43.390
Do any of the panelists have any questions

00:34:43.390 --> 00:34:48.390
or comments inspired by your
colleagues presentations?

00:34:52.000 --> 00:34:53.970
- Well, I have a question that echoes

00:34:53.970 --> 00:34:57.200
one that was asked in the Q
and A that I can see here,

00:34:57.200 --> 00:34:59.630
from the attendees which is to Fiona,

00:34:59.630 --> 00:35:01.340
which is just what you think,

00:35:01.340 --> 00:35:02.750
if anything is gonna take place

00:35:02.750 --> 00:35:04.520
in the healthcare antitrust arena?

00:35:06.740 --> 00:35:09.650
- I think that there'll
be vigorous enforcement,

00:35:09.650 --> 00:35:11.920
relative to the last four years.

00:35:11.920 --> 00:35:13.710
And that might show up,

00:35:15.730 --> 00:35:18.990
if you give the FTC more money,
that will show up in pharma,

00:35:18.990 --> 00:35:21.780
that will show up in hospitals
and hospital mergers,

00:35:22.660 --> 00:35:27.660
that might actually appear also
a little bit in regulations,

00:35:28.090 --> 00:35:31.810
that have up til now
actually protected incumbents

00:35:31.810 --> 00:35:34.260
from competition instead
of exposing incumbents

00:35:34.260 --> 00:35:35.180
to competition.

00:35:36.930 --> 00:35:41.110
So, I don't think it's
gonna do what your remarks

00:35:41.110 --> 00:35:41.943
talked about, Jacob,

00:35:41.943 --> 00:35:46.420
which is really giving you no
more access to more people,

00:35:46.420 --> 00:35:48.830
but every little bit counts.

00:35:48.830 --> 00:35:50.990
And the more market
power every doctor group,

00:35:50.990 --> 00:35:54.300
or hospital group or drug
has means that especially,

00:35:54.300 --> 00:35:58.920
as we see insurers and employers
shifting more of the costs

00:35:58.920 --> 00:36:01.350
directly on to enrollees,

00:36:01.350 --> 00:36:03.890
they're going out and paying
these out-of-pocket prices,

00:36:03.890 --> 00:36:05.480
that keep climbing.

00:36:05.480 --> 00:36:10.320
So, I think it is an important
way to help regular citizens

00:36:10.320 --> 00:36:11.840
by lowering those costs.

00:36:15.430 --> 00:36:16.263
- Thanks.

00:36:19.490 --> 00:36:23.080
- Good, any other comments from panelists,

