what is can you unpack
that a little bit as well?

00:40:21.790 --> 00:40:25.930
- So, I find it's interesting
Fiona the framing that,

00:40:25.930 --> 00:40:27.620
sort of voting rights above all else,

00:40:27.620 --> 00:40:30.070
because without people who
are sympathetic to this,

00:40:30.070 --> 00:40:32.490
we don't get any other things done.

00:40:32.490 --> 00:40:35.960
There's a part of me, both
in my brain and in my life,

00:40:35.960 --> 00:40:39.990
that is committed to
that understanding of it.

00:40:39.990 --> 00:40:44.160
And yet also, I don't think
anyone who was elected

00:40:44.160 --> 00:40:47.740
to federal office prior to May 25th,

00:40:47.740 --> 00:40:50.320
would have been discussing
police abolition,

00:40:50.320 --> 00:40:52.760
without the movements
that are outside of this.

00:40:52.760 --> 00:40:54.100
So, sometimes it's the case,

00:40:54.100 --> 00:40:56.890
that what you want, if
you wanna get the better

00:40:56.890 --> 00:40:59.750
of two or three or four options,

00:40:59.750 --> 00:41:01.100
into the elected seat,

00:41:01.100 --> 00:41:03.220
but sometimes you just need
to change the conditions

00:41:03.220 --> 00:41:06.220
on the ground to make it unreasonable,

00:41:06.220 --> 00:41:08.520
that there wouldn't be a
movement going forward.

00:41:08.520 --> 00:41:11.820
You had Republicans in the
Senate judiciary hearing,

00:41:11.820 --> 00:41:13.840
on issues of policing,

00:41:13.840 --> 00:41:17.250
calling folks like me
to come talk to them,

00:41:17.250 --> 00:41:19.190
about racism in policing,

00:41:19.190 --> 00:41:21.630
an area that's not their issue,

00:41:21.630 --> 00:41:23.490
but you couldn't see what was happening

00:41:23.490 --> 00:41:26.670
across the United States
and the public protests

00:41:26.670 --> 00:41:28.820
in Thailand, right?

00:41:28.820 --> 00:41:31.610
Call the science and say
Black Lives Matter and say,

00:41:31.610 --> 00:41:33.630
we should do nothing.

00:41:33.630 --> 00:41:36.980
So, my hope is that,
the kind of engagement,

00:41:36.980 --> 00:41:38.150
and activism that was spawned,

00:41:38.150 --> 00:41:40.320
both of being locked inside,

00:41:40.320 --> 00:41:42.360
for those first couple of months,

00:41:42.360 --> 00:41:46.270
and of seeing a world that
doesn't look like our values,

00:41:46.270 --> 00:41:47.720
playing out on the streets,

00:41:47.720 --> 00:41:49.820
of what does look like our communities,

00:41:49.820 --> 00:41:52.950
I'm hoping that engagement
stays up, right?

00:41:52.950 --> 00:41:55.980
So, it's the case that because
I get to teach undergraduates

00:41:55.980 --> 00:41:57.920
and I occasionally talk
with high school students,

00:41:57.920 --> 00:42:00.860
I deal with not only people who
have a lot more hair than me

00:42:00.860 --> 00:42:02.620
but people with a lot more energy,

00:42:02.620 --> 00:42:05.420
and a lot more devotion to these issues.

00:42:05.420 --> 00:42:08.530
I have not seen in my adult life,

00:42:08.530 --> 00:42:10.400
the level, not just a
political engagement,

00:42:10.400 --> 00:42:12.960
but a political
sophistication and education,

00:42:12.960 --> 00:42:16.010
and the desire to take a long form view.

00:42:16.010 --> 00:42:19.490
So, what I mean Alan, to
keeping the pressure on,

00:42:19.490 --> 00:42:21.410
when Obama was leaving office, he said,

00:42:21.410 --> 00:42:24.440
you guys could have pushed
me to do more, right.

00:42:24.440 --> 00:42:27.350
And there's the famous bit both FDR,

00:42:27.350 --> 00:42:29.780
there's a version of
FDR doing and LBJ doing,

00:42:29.780 --> 00:42:32.000
of like, I agree with you
now, go make me do it, right.

00:42:32.000 --> 00:42:33.220
It's after you are first, right.

00:42:33.220 --> 00:42:34.460
But the idea is that,

00:42:35.750 --> 00:42:40.750
it's possible to move the
center of political will,

00:42:41.040 --> 00:42:43.290
outside of the electoral process.

00:42:43.290 --> 00:42:44.850
And I think it's really
important to name that,

00:42:44.850 --> 00:42:47.130
because in the communities where I work,

00:42:47.130 --> 00:42:48.550
there are lots of folks who feel like,

00:42:48.550 --> 00:42:50.100
the game's already rigged.

00:42:50.100 --> 00:42:52.130
There's nobody who's gonna
be passing any legislation

00:42:52.130 --> 00:42:53.550
that speaks to their issues,

00:42:53.550 --> 00:42:55.640
that make their lives easier,
that make it more likely

00:42:55.640 --> 00:42:58.710
that their proximity of the
American dream gets any closer.

00:42:59.720 --> 00:43:01.220
But I do know how to take to the streets,

00:43:01.220 --> 00:43:03.480
I do know how to write a sign.

00:43:03.480 --> 00:43:05.990
And the more we have folks
who are able to speak to them

00:43:05.990 --> 00:43:07.690
the less that's necessary,

00:43:07.690 --> 00:43:11.850
the more they engage in
those kinds of extra partisan

00:43:11.850 --> 00:43:13.940
behaviors, right,

00:43:13.940 --> 00:43:15.270
the more we're gonna
get people who are gonna

00:43:15.270 --> 00:43:17.120
be stuck closer to them.

00:43:17.120 --> 00:43:20.460
So, my hope is that we get
significantly more of that.

00:43:20.460 --> 00:43:22.920
And at the same time that
we're expanding voting rights,

00:43:22.920 --> 00:43:24.420
we have folks who
understand that elections,

00:43:24.420 --> 00:43:27.160
are not the only kind of
politics in the country.

00:43:27.160 --> 00:43:27.993
- Good.

00:43:27.993 --> 00:43:28.920
- Separate from that,

00:43:28.920 --> 00:43:30.940
can I pivot and ask a
question back to the panel

00:43:30.940 --> 00:43:31.800
as we move through?

00:43:31.800 --> 00:43:34.550
- Yeah, of course.

00:43:34.550 --> 00:43:36.780
- Every time I listen to
my brilliant colleagues

00:43:36.780 --> 00:43:37.860
in other areas,

00:43:37.860 --> 00:43:40.570
I'm so eager to just go and take classes

00:43:40.570 --> 00:43:43.610
and also to be the selfish
person in the class who says,

00:43:43.610 --> 00:43:45.190
how does that relate to my thing?

00:43:45.190 --> 00:43:48.360
'Cause I'm thinking increasingly about,

00:43:48.360 --> 00:43:50.750
the social determinants of safety.

00:43:50.750 --> 00:43:52.440
The way we talk about the
social determinants of health.

00:43:52.440 --> 00:43:54.080
There isn't a science of this.

00:43:54.080 --> 00:43:55.000
We're trying to build it up,

00:43:55.000 --> 00:43:56.670
because we were just not invested,

00:43:56.670 --> 00:43:59.480
but I'm interested both
from Jacob and Fiona,

00:43:59.480 --> 00:44:03.090
if you guys can talk about the
ways that you see synergies

00:44:03.090 --> 00:44:05.640
across the federal government,
in what you're doing.

00:44:05.640 --> 00:44:07.970
'Cause when I talk about a reckoning,

00:44:07.970 --> 00:44:10.260
I talked about DOJ looking back,

00:44:10.260 --> 00:44:13.470
but it's also HUD that
needs to look back, right?

00:44:13.470 --> 00:44:15.720
And it's absolutely like
when we're talking about,

00:44:15.720 --> 00:44:17.290
privacy and surveillance,

00:44:17.290 --> 00:44:19.240
we're gonna have to talk about antitrust.

00:44:19.240 --> 00:44:22.610
So, in all of the areas where
you guys have expertise,

00:44:22.610 --> 00:44:25.170
they're not even two degrees removed,

00:44:25.170 --> 00:44:28.100
from the spaces that if we
do things before policing,

00:44:28.100 --> 00:44:29.420
to take care of public safety,

00:44:29.420 --> 00:44:30.520
we're going to need to talk.

00:44:30.520 --> 00:44:34.360
But part our blockages,
the obstacles is that,

00:44:34.360 --> 00:44:37.960
these issue areas become
silos within government,

00:44:37.960 --> 00:44:39.800
and there's not been conversation.

00:44:39.800 --> 00:44:43.670
So, in this summer have
something different for everyone,

00:44:43.670 --> 00:44:45.890
I'm wondering the degree to
which you've been able to talk

00:44:45.890 --> 00:44:48.560
to folks in other areas,

00:44:48.560 --> 00:44:51.120
about what you feel like
needs to move forward,

00:44:51.120 --> 00:44:53.430
and the ways in which we
can start to break down,

00:44:53.430 --> 00:44:55.680
some of those silos to
get more accomplished.

00:44:57.900 --> 00:44:59.350
- I have one answer.

00:45:00.970 --> 00:45:02.330
At the Thurman Arnold Project,

00:45:02.330 --> 00:45:05.170
we had a conference
halfway through January,

00:45:05.170 --> 00:45:07.030
which you can go look up on the web,

00:45:08.130 --> 00:45:11.930
entitled antitrust and
big AG, big agriculture.

00:45:12.860 --> 00:45:16.610
And one of the things
that is just astonishing,

00:45:16.610 --> 00:45:18.510
I knew relatively little about agriculture

00:45:18.510 --> 00:45:20.490
before we started down this path,

00:45:20.490 --> 00:45:24.070
the conference was run
organized primarily,

00:45:24.070 --> 00:45:26.270
by my deputy Austin Ferric.

00:45:26.270 --> 00:45:30.860
And when you go and look at
the problems in rural America,

00:45:30.860 --> 00:45:33.910
not only do we have
incredible market power,

00:45:33.910 --> 00:45:37.140
by slaughterhouses and meat packers,

00:45:37.140 --> 00:45:39.930
and the guys who buy the beef and the lamb

00:45:39.930 --> 00:45:42.050
and sell the tractors and the grain,

00:45:42.050 --> 00:45:43.910
Monsanto and John Deere and so on,

00:45:44.880 --> 00:45:46.270
who are they employing?

00:45:46.270 --> 00:45:50.240
They're employing black and
brown workers, immigrants,

00:45:50.240 --> 00:45:51.580
they're not paying them,

00:45:51.580 --> 00:45:53.210
the working conditions are terrible.

00:45:53.210 --> 00:45:54.043
Why is that?

00:45:54.043 --> 00:45:55.800
Because they have monopsony power.

00:45:55.800 --> 00:45:57.660
They're the only employer in the town,

00:45:57.660 --> 00:45:59.560
not only are the only seller of meat.

00:45:59.560 --> 00:46:01.560
So, the meat price is high,

00:46:01.560 --> 00:46:03.060
the wages are low,

00:46:03.060 --> 00:46:05.880
the environmental
degradation is really high.

00:46:05.880 --> 00:46:08.520
The rural community is then hollowed out,

00:46:08.520 --> 00:46:11.890
because the jobs are poor
and the air quality is awful.

00:46:11.890 --> 00:46:14.500
The animals are unhappy
and badly cared for.

00:46:14.500 --> 00:46:17.100
So, it's a confluence of places

00:46:17.100 --> 00:46:18.510
where you don't wanna look at it,

00:46:18.510 --> 00:46:20.250
just as the Department of Agriculture,

00:46:20.250 --> 00:46:22.800
or the Department of Justice or the EPA.

00:46:22.800 --> 00:46:25.580
You want to say all of these
things are going wrong,

00:46:25.580 --> 00:46:28.510
in these rural communities
at the same time.

00:46:28.510 --> 00:46:30.100
And if we went in and we looked

00:46:30.100 --> 00:46:31.410
at how concentrated they are,

00:46:31.410 --> 00:46:35.350
and we tried to make there
be more choice for employers,

00:46:35.350 --> 00:46:37.300
if there were an environmental regulation

00:46:37.300 --> 00:46:39.990
that limited the size of hog farms,

00:46:39.990 --> 00:46:41.840
that would reduce air pollution,

00:46:41.840 --> 00:46:44.760
it would create more
employers for the workers,

00:46:44.760 --> 00:46:46.390
it would improve the lives of the animals.

00:46:46.390 --> 00:46:47.400
I mean, just everything,

00:46:47.400 --> 00:46:50.630
it's like one bird and sorry,
one stone and eight birds.

00:46:51.700 --> 00:46:53.350
And that's a place where I think,

00:46:53.350 --> 00:46:54.800
the silos need to break down.

00:46:56.030 --> 00:46:56.960
- And I wanna jump,

00:46:56.960 --> 00:46:57.900
I wanna get to the questions

00:46:57.900 --> 00:47:01.180
but let me just say the
obvious Phil which is that,

00:47:01.180 --> 00:47:03.210
we're not talking about
healthcare the same way we were

00:47:03.210 --> 00:47:06.060
before the pandemic or before
the protests of the summer.

00:47:07.110 --> 00:47:11.210
The degree to which we, people
who are on the front lines

00:47:11.210 --> 00:47:13.890
thinking through healthcare
are having to confront

00:47:13.890 --> 00:47:16.710
the profound racial inequities,

00:47:16.710 --> 00:47:19.080
and class inequities that
are embedded in the system,

00:47:19.080 --> 00:47:21.090
that would not be fixed,

00:47:21.090 --> 00:47:24.920
were by conventional
reforms if we were not,

00:47:24.920 --> 00:47:27.930
if we don't confront those
inequities frontally,

00:47:27.930 --> 00:47:30.210
that is just a sea change
in thinking that I think,

00:47:30.210 --> 00:47:32.320
in the longterm will
deliver a lot of good.

00:47:32.320 --> 00:47:33.920
Unfortunately, in the near term,

00:47:35.070 --> 00:47:37.080
this has just been a
heartbreaking reminder.

00:47:37.080 --> 00:47:38.900
This period has been a
heartbreaking reminder,

00:47:38.900 --> 00:47:43.900
of how HUN finished that the
agenda of systemic reform.

00:47:45.060 --> 00:47:46.430
And it really is.

00:47:46.430 --> 00:47:49.100
So, that would be my quick answer.

00:47:50.090 --> 00:47:52.010
- Okay, good.

00:47:52.010 --> 00:47:52.990
All right, thank you.

00:47:52.990 --> 00:47:56.280
Those are really extremely
interesting remarks.

00:47:56.280 --> 00:47:58.420
Let me turn now to some of the questions,

00:47:59.528 --> 00:48:02.910
that our audience has been has asked

00:48:02.910 --> 00:48:05.340
and is continuing to ask.

00:48:05.340 --> 00:48:08.450
I'm just gonna sorta read a few of them,

00:48:08.450 --> 00:48:11.810
and then get some answers
from the panelist.

00:48:12.890 --> 00:48:15.030
Phil, there was a question for you,

00:48:15.030 --> 00:48:16.570
that like I'm having trouble finding,

00:48:16.570 --> 00:48:20.220
but it was a very specific question about,

00:48:20.220 --> 00:48:23.700
so, suppose you were to
reduce the money spent

00:48:23.700 --> 00:48:26.810
on the police force and redirected

00:48:26.810 --> 00:48:29.180
into some community spending,

00:48:29.180 --> 00:48:34.180
with an effort to improve
the quality of life

00:48:34.950 --> 00:48:36.520
in the effected communities,

00:48:37.690 --> 00:48:39.980
what would be the best way in your view,

00:48:39.980 --> 00:48:43.120
to spend that money for that purpose?

00:48:43.120 --> 00:48:45.040
And I don't know if you have
any thoughts about that.

00:48:45.040 --> 00:48:48.040
I know you've been very involved
in some of these efforts.

00:48:48.900 --> 00:48:51.470
- Yeah, you can't find it,
'cause I typed an answer,

00:48:51.470 --> 00:48:53.400
so, it showed up in the answer section.

00:48:53.400 --> 00:48:54.510
No, but it's okay.

00:48:54.510 --> 00:48:56.910
I'm very happy to speak
a portion of it as well.

00:48:57.900 --> 00:48:58.900
It's a great question.

00:48:58.900 --> 00:49:00.360
I have been thinking a great deal about,

00:49:00.360 --> 00:49:04.520
how do we do redesign instead
of just re-imagining this.

00:49:04.520 --> 00:49:06.110
At some point you got to
actually do some things,

00:49:06.110 --> 00:49:07.610
'cause there's bodies souling.

00:49:08.500 --> 00:49:10.160
And I think it also goes to,

00:49:10.160 --> 00:49:11.920
there was another question
in the chat about,

00:49:11.920 --> 00:49:15.370
the trade-off between short term reforms,

00:49:15.370 --> 00:49:16.810
getting what you can,

00:49:16.810 --> 00:49:19.810
and the longer term of getting
to where we need to go.

00:49:21.555 --> 00:49:25.850
I'll say that I have
been in a bunch of spaces

00:49:25.850 --> 00:49:30.020
where mayors and governors
and State reps are saying,

00:49:30.020 --> 00:49:34.510
we don't know how to get
from here to a smaller,

00:49:34.510 --> 00:49:36.090
a police force size.

00:49:36.090 --> 00:49:38.900
We don't know how to
get from here to better.

00:49:38.900 --> 00:49:41.870
So, we need a bunch of
research before we move.

00:49:42.880 --> 00:49:44.550
Now, I'm a scientist, right?

00:49:44.550 --> 00:49:46.420
I'm in favor of more research,

00:49:46.420 --> 00:49:49.000
that nobody who wants more data
on public safety than I do.

00:49:49.000 --> 00:49:51.600
I swear I would challenge
any of you to fight on it.

00:49:53.160 --> 00:49:56.010
But that's not to say we
need to do the science first.

00:49:56.010 --> 00:49:58.810
Sometimes, the science
has to come afterwards.

00:49:58.810 --> 00:50:01.410
And there are literally
thousands of natural experiments,

00:50:01.410 --> 00:50:03.460
happening all over the country right now.

00:50:04.310 --> 00:50:07.850
I don't know what the right
percentage of budget is,

00:50:07.850 --> 00:50:09.560
to take away from law enforcement,

00:50:09.560 --> 00:50:12.450
and put into mental health
versus substance abuse,

00:50:12.450 --> 00:50:14.770
versus child welfare
versus green greenspaces,

00:50:14.770 --> 00:50:16.210
versus food insecurity.

00:50:16.210 --> 00:50:19.400
I know that each of those things
are really good predictors

00:50:19.400 --> 00:50:22.620
of safety and of police
intervention later on.

00:50:22.620 --> 00:50:26.690
And so, the more money you've
got in social services,

00:50:26.690 --> 00:50:28.740
particularly for mental
health and substance abuse,

00:50:28.740 --> 00:50:31.040
the less likely you are to be calling 911.

00:50:31.040 --> 00:50:35.600
And in cities like San Francisco
and Oakland and Austin,

00:50:35.600 --> 00:50:36.433
where they have,

00:50:36.433 --> 00:50:38.660
and Houston now where
they've taken law enforcement

00:50:38.660 --> 00:50:40.910
and segregated out the
mental health response.

00:50:40.910 --> 00:50:42.830
So, it's just social workers.

00:50:42.830 --> 00:50:45.040
You're seeing a two
and three and in Austin

00:50:45.040 --> 00:50:49.110
a four X response to 911.

00:50:49.110 --> 00:50:52.620
911 calls go up four
times on certain weekends,

00:50:52.620 --> 00:50:53.650
just because they know they can get

00:50:53.650 --> 00:50:55.090
a mental health response.

00:50:55.090 --> 00:50:57.120
That suggests a huge under utilization

00:50:57.120 --> 00:50:58.720
of mental health resources,

00:50:58.720 --> 00:51:02.330
which is an unintended
insight of this moment,

00:51:02.330 --> 00:51:04.480
where we're having this
reckoning with policing.

00:51:04.480 --> 00:51:05.490
But the long and short is,

00:51:05.490 --> 00:51:07.910
we don't know exactly how much,

00:51:07.910 --> 00:51:10.970
so what we should be doing
is a longterm investment

00:51:10.970 --> 00:51:12.340
in science,

00:51:12.340 --> 00:51:14.350
so that all of these natural experiments,

00:51:14.350 --> 00:51:16.940
are yielding information some insights,

00:51:16.940 --> 00:51:19.230
into how we think about scaling.

00:51:19.230 --> 00:51:21.650
And to the person who
asked about short-term

00:51:21.650 --> 00:51:22.540
versus long-term.

00:51:22.540 --> 00:51:24.450
I know that Jacob and Fiona
have things to say about it,

00:51:24.450 --> 00:51:27.290
and I wanna be out of the
way so they can answer.

00:51:27.290 --> 00:51:30.200
But in the context of
policing, it is often the case.

00:51:30.200 --> 00:51:31.120
Well, we can't get to that,

00:51:31.120 --> 00:51:32.850
so, let's do this small thing,

00:51:32.850 --> 00:51:34.550
has been the way that movements

00:51:34.550 --> 00:51:36.370
and requests for progress die.

00:51:37.660 --> 00:51:39.200
The real answer to this I think is,

00:51:39.200 --> 00:51:42.440
we have to have a shared
idea about where we're going.

00:51:42.440 --> 00:51:46.550
That way if I'm saying we want
to abolish the death penalty

00:51:46.550 --> 00:51:47.410
that's not in the way

00:51:47.410 --> 00:51:49.360
of shrinking the size of law enforcement.

00:51:49.360 --> 00:51:51.590
And if we're saying, we
wanna give feedback back,

00:51:51.590 --> 00:51:53.740
on racial equity and police behavior,

00:51:53.740 --> 00:51:55.190
that's not in the way.

00:51:55.190 --> 00:51:56.830
There even some trainings
that are not in the way,

00:51:56.830 --> 00:51:59.820
provided they don't create
a whole economic system,

00:51:59.820 --> 00:52:01.870
that feeds the beast of unions,

00:52:01.870 --> 00:52:04.440
and out of control growth
in law enforcement.

00:52:04.440 --> 00:52:05.780
But without that shared understanding

00:52:05.780 --> 00:52:08.890
of where we're going
without the roadmap, right,

00:52:08.890 --> 00:52:10.850
my what we can get now is in the way

00:52:10.850 --> 00:52:12.920
of someone else's revolution.

00:52:12.920 --> 00:52:13.753
And too often,

00:52:13.753 --> 00:52:16.550
when we're trying to combine
movements with science,

00:52:16.550 --> 00:52:18.930
smart policy to try and
save people's lives,

00:52:18.930 --> 00:52:21.620
with a broader sense of
what democracy can be,

00:52:21.620 --> 00:52:23.260
that's where we end up
cannibalizing each other,

00:52:23.260 --> 00:52:24.690
and getting absolutely nothing,

00:52:24.690 --> 00:52:26.560
which by the way is what
it feels like we got,

00:52:26.560 --> 00:52:28.990
each and every single time we've
had a reckoning on policing

00:52:28.990 --> 00:52:32.460
prior to that, what we
got for Michael Brown Jr,

00:52:32.460 --> 00:52:36.190
what we got for Rodney King,

00:52:36.190 --> 00:52:39.710
what we got in 1968,
what we got after Tulsa.

00:52:39.710 --> 00:52:41.210
- Good, thank you, Phil.

00:52:41.210 --> 00:52:46.147
Let me ask Jacob a question,
Jacob, what would you say,

00:52:47.280 --> 00:52:49.900
so, I can certainly imagine,

00:52:49.900 --> 00:52:53.460
and I I heard your arguments for why,

00:52:53.460 --> 00:52:58.460
it's a moral imperative to
expand the healthcare coverage.

00:52:59.010 --> 00:53:02.550
I find those very compelling personally,

00:53:02.550 --> 00:53:06.110
what would you say is the
best argument against,

00:53:06.110 --> 00:53:08.030
the public option?

00:53:08.030 --> 00:53:11.850
So, as this debate unfolds,
what do you anticipate?

00:53:11.850 --> 00:53:14.300
I mean, not what will be,

00:53:14.300 --> 00:53:17.070
I mean I guess I could ask
you what arguments you think

00:53:17.070 --> 00:53:20.410
will be made against it when it comes up,

00:53:20.410 --> 00:53:21.940
but what do you think of as some of,

00:53:21.940 --> 00:53:24.120
what are the arguments against it,

00:53:24.120 --> 00:53:26.600
that you caused you to pause at all?

00:53:27.500 --> 00:53:30.580
Are any of them reasonable
in your view or?

00:53:31.624 --> 00:53:35.680
- Yeah, no, I mean, look, if
I wouldn't be a good academic

00:53:35.680 --> 00:53:38.840
if I wasn't racked with self-doubt right.

00:53:38.840 --> 00:53:42.600
So, no, I mean, I recognize
that the public option

00:53:42.600 --> 00:53:45.740
is not to be all end all
of healthcare reform,

00:53:46.730 --> 00:53:48.630
but I think that,

00:53:48.630 --> 00:53:51.820
and let me just say about
what I consider to be the kind

00:53:51.820 --> 00:53:53.510
of important developments
and thinking about

00:53:53.510 --> 00:53:55.130
the public option that have taken place

00:53:55.130 --> 00:53:59.020
since the idea first emerged
on the public agenda,

00:53:59.020 --> 00:54:03.311
during the debate over the
Affordable Care Act in 2009, 10.

00:54:03.311 --> 00:54:06.510
So, during that discussion,

00:54:06.510 --> 00:54:08.460
I think the public option was seen as,

00:54:08.460 --> 00:54:11.800
mostly a kind of tacked on addition

00:54:11.800 --> 00:54:15.700
to a reform framework that
mostly followed the model

00:54:15.700 --> 00:54:18.790
that had been passed in
Massachusetts, right?

00:54:18.790 --> 00:54:22.300
Where people would have
access to private insurance,

00:54:22.300 --> 00:54:24.680
and that would be regulated.

00:54:24.680 --> 00:54:26.170
And the trade off was essentially,

00:54:26.170 --> 00:54:27.710
or the bargain was essentially,

00:54:27.710 --> 00:54:30.780
that the insurance companies
would essentially agree

00:54:30.780 --> 00:54:33.670
to be regulated and not
be able to discriminate

00:54:33.670 --> 00:54:36.470
against unhealthy patients
or charge them higher rates,

00:54:36.470 --> 00:54:39.460
in return they would basically
get a bundle of money,

00:54:39.460 --> 00:54:44.190
from government and to
provide that coverage,

00:54:44.190 --> 00:54:47.800
and the forces of kind of competition,

00:54:47.800 --> 00:54:49.860
choosing among these plans,

00:54:49.860 --> 00:54:52.020
would basically bring down costs,

00:54:52.020 --> 00:54:53.420
for that coverage over time.

00:54:54.310 --> 00:54:55.780
It was seen as ancillary

00:54:55.780 --> 00:54:58.212
to a fundamentally
employment-based system.

00:54:58.212 --> 00:55:00.730
And so, that was never my vision,

00:55:00.730 --> 00:55:03.890
but that was really
where it was in 2009, 10.

00:55:03.890 --> 00:55:05.350
And if you look at where it's gone,

00:55:05.350 --> 00:55:08.380
and I think if you put a
public option into that system,

00:55:08.380 --> 00:55:13.090
it's basically, it's very likely
to get marginalized, right?

00:55:14.300 --> 00:55:15.960
And so, where the debate is,

00:55:15.960 --> 00:55:17.120
where I think the debate has gone

00:55:17.120 --> 00:55:18.570
and needs to continue to go,

00:55:18.570 --> 00:55:21.080
is the idea that the
public insurance option,

00:55:21.080 --> 00:55:22.500
should be something quite distinct

00:55:22.500 --> 00:55:24.920
from private insurance plans.

00:55:24.920 --> 00:55:27.110
It should be modeled after Medicare.

00:55:27.110 --> 00:55:29.910
It should be public in the sense that,

00:55:29.910 --> 00:55:32.940
it's pulling the risks with
the federal government,

00:55:33.810 --> 00:55:36.270
essentially managing it
and paying the costs.

00:55:36.270 --> 00:55:39.670
It should have a broad
access to providers,

00:55:39.670 --> 00:55:42.360
and it should use regulated payment rates.

00:55:43.230 --> 00:55:46.570
And that it should be
something that is available,

00:55:46.570 --> 00:55:49.130
not just to those who don't
have coverage to their employer

00:55:49.130 --> 00:55:51.267
but to those who have
coverage from their employer,

00:55:51.267 --> 00:55:54.890
that doesn't provide adequate protection.

00:55:54.890 --> 00:55:58.270
And so, in that world, I
think the public option,

00:55:58.270 --> 00:56:00.220
has a lot more transformative potential.

00:56:00.220 --> 00:56:03.880
I mean, on one side, you'll
have critics conservative saying

00:56:03.880 --> 00:56:08.190
it's gonna be a slippery
slope to universal Medicare

00:56:09.434 --> 00:56:10.267
on the end.

00:56:10.267 --> 00:56:12.300
And many advocates might
think that as well.

00:56:12.300 --> 00:56:14.550
And on the other side,
you're gonna have Medicare

00:56:14.550 --> 00:56:16.250
for all advocates saying,

00:56:16.250 --> 00:56:19.300
if you have that system it's
not gonna be robust enough

00:56:19.300 --> 00:56:22.560
to be able to provide
the kind of protection

00:56:22.560 --> 00:56:23.960
that citizens need,

00:56:23.960 --> 00:56:26.020
that you could get through
a universal system,

00:56:26.020 --> 00:56:28.160
and the insurance
companies have figured out,

00:56:28.160 --> 00:56:31.040
how to game Medicare, they'll
figure out how to game it.

00:56:32.696 --> 00:56:35.460
And to me, so, that's I think the vision

00:56:35.460 --> 00:56:37.300
has really evolved quite a bit,

00:56:37.300 --> 00:56:42.300
into something where the
public option would be seen

00:56:42.350 --> 00:56:45.390
as something universally
available nationwide,

00:56:45.390 --> 00:56:47.780
to everyone regardless of
whether they had coverage,

00:56:47.780 --> 00:56:49.210
through another source,

00:56:49.210 --> 00:56:52.780
and that would be built
on a public platform.

00:56:52.780 --> 00:56:55.590
But I do think there
are real risks even then

00:56:55.590 --> 00:56:58.810
because and Fiona knows this well,

00:56:58.810 --> 00:57:01.730
the fundamental reality is
that if we don't figure out

00:57:01.730 --> 00:57:05.050
how to provide coverage affordably, right?

00:57:05.050 --> 00:57:07.990
Any platform we build,
isn't gonna be sustainable.

00:57:07.990 --> 00:57:11.330
And that's the path we're on today.

00:57:11.330 --> 00:57:13.440
It's the path we've been on.

00:57:13.440 --> 00:57:16.720
And I still think there's
not enough guarantee,

00:57:16.720 --> 00:57:19.150
in current models to be
able to really provide

00:57:19.150 --> 00:57:23.010
that kind of coverage on
generous terms over time.

00:57:23.010 --> 00:57:25.260
And so, to me, that's where a lot of

00:57:25.260 --> 00:57:29.500
the most significant thinking
is gonna need to take place.

00:57:29.500 --> 00:57:33.220
And one thing the pandemic I think

00:57:33.220 --> 00:57:34.780
has really driven home is that,

00:57:35.980 --> 00:57:38.550
is it like a lot of the
things we took for granted

00:57:38.550 --> 00:57:40.240
about what works in healthcare,

00:57:42.290 --> 00:57:46.360
are pretty fragile and the
kind of the models of payment,

00:57:46.360 --> 00:57:49.020
and delivery of care that we have,

00:57:49.020 --> 00:57:51.340
that have developed the United States,

00:57:51.340 --> 00:57:53.020
can produce deeply,

00:57:53.020 --> 00:57:54.810
well, it can produce
really wonderful outcomes

00:57:54.810 --> 00:57:56.750
produced deeply perverse ones as well.

00:57:56.750 --> 00:57:59.810
And so, to me, that's gonna
be the sort of next frontier,

00:57:59.810 --> 00:58:02.130
of thinking about the public option.

00:58:02.130 --> 00:58:03.490
- Great, thanks very much.

00:58:03.490 --> 00:58:05.440
That's very interesting Jacob.

00:58:06.430 --> 00:58:09.470
A question that I'd like to send to Fiona,

00:58:10.580 --> 00:58:15.580
a question on the questionnaire
asks you to comment

00:58:16.920 --> 00:58:19.680
on the Facebook oversight
board as a protector

00:58:19.680 --> 00:58:23.850
for consumers, rather than
an obstacle for competitors.

00:58:23.850 --> 00:58:27.900
And let me just pivot on that
a little bit and just say,

00:58:27.900 --> 00:58:29.880
what do you think about the general idea,

00:58:29.880 --> 00:58:34.880
of self-regulation by these large tech,

00:58:35.300 --> 00:58:39.320
by these tech giants says as
a regulatory strategy to you?

00:58:39.320 --> 00:58:41.550
'Cause that seems to be at least,

00:58:41.550 --> 00:58:45.190
I think that's sort of the
impulse I think of that,

00:58:46.500 --> 00:58:50.520
Facebook Supreme Court, I
guess is that they've set up.

00:58:51.950 --> 00:58:55.670
- Well, many decades, perhaps centuries,

00:58:55.670 --> 00:58:58.240
companies have been in
favor of self regulation,

00:58:58.240 --> 00:59:01.540
and we know why, because it doesn't work.

00:59:01.540 --> 00:59:04.290
I mean, if you have a
profit incentive to engage

00:59:04.290 --> 00:59:06.960
in a certain behavior
that harms consumers,

00:59:06.960 --> 00:59:08.640
saying you promise not to do it,

00:59:08.640 --> 00:59:10.520
is not gonna be a very full-proof way

00:59:10.520 --> 00:59:11.670
of getting that outcome.

00:59:11.670 --> 00:59:14.280
So, I think in all seriousness,

00:59:14.280 --> 00:59:16.610
this is a terrible idea
and it never works.

00:59:16.610 --> 00:59:19.490
And if societies want certain outcomes,

00:59:19.490 --> 00:59:22.430
then they should codify them
in law and make a regulator,

00:59:22.430 --> 00:59:24.780
and be serious about holding companies

00:59:24.780 --> 00:59:26.980
to the standards of
behavior that they want.

00:59:28.480 --> 00:59:30.720
I think when people ask me about,

00:59:30.720 --> 00:59:33.750
difficult first amendment
questions and speech

00:59:33.750 --> 00:59:38.020
and censorship and whatever
about the digital platforms,

00:59:38.020 --> 00:59:42.970
my usual answer is to say
that's not my area of expertise,

00:59:42.970 --> 00:59:45.940
go see professor Jack
Balkan at the law school,

00:59:45.940 --> 00:59:49.990
but also, boy wouldn't it be nice,

00:59:49.990 --> 00:59:52.530
if I could change my social network,

00:59:52.530 --> 00:59:54.530
when I didn't like what it was doing.

00:59:54.530 --> 00:59:56.060
I have a newspaper, if I don't,

00:59:56.060 --> 00:59:58.770
if I decided I didn't like
the content of my newspaper,

00:59:58.770 --> 01:00:01.200
I will subscribed to
a different newspaper.

01:00:01.200 --> 01:00:02.670
The problem that we have with a lot

01:00:02.670 --> 01:00:06.010
of these digital platforms,
is there isn't another choice.

01:00:06.010 --> 01:00:08.680
So, I feel like if we
could get the antitrust,

01:00:08.680 --> 01:00:10.710
and the competition
policy and the regulation

01:00:10.710 --> 01:00:13.330
and the entry barriers to work better,

01:00:13.330 --> 01:00:16.070
so, there was more entry
and more competition

01:00:16.070 --> 01:00:18.270
and therefore more choices,

01:00:18.270 --> 01:00:22.490
that the fraught question of
how to regulate the content

01:00:22.490 --> 01:00:24.650
of the platform would become lessened,

01:00:24.650 --> 01:00:27.210
because people would
simply vote with their feet

01:00:27.210 --> 01:00:28.880
and go somewhere else.

01:00:28.880 --> 01:00:31.120
And there would still be issues,

01:00:31.120 --> 01:00:35.000
but many many people
wouldn't feel as frustrated,

01:00:35.000 --> 01:00:36.620
because they would be able to move

01:00:36.620 --> 01:00:40.230
to a place that was giving
them what they wanted.

01:00:41.160 --> 01:00:44.180
- Fiona another question I
just wanted to throw to you,

01:00:45.100 --> 01:00:48.850
about regulating the tech companies.

01:00:48.850 --> 01:00:50.350
Well, I'll read the question,

01:00:51.440 --> 01:00:55.220
information technologies
do not map to classical,

01:00:55.220 --> 01:00:58.260
antitrust conception of bundled products

01:00:58.260 --> 01:00:59.810
information in its collection,

01:00:59.810 --> 01:01:02.280
permits the creation of services,

01:01:02.280 --> 01:01:05.110
that are simply not
available to small startups,

01:01:05.110 --> 01:01:08.200
in particular, the social
graph like the human genome

01:01:08.200 --> 01:01:10.650
is a feature of the natural world.

01:01:10.650 --> 01:01:13.690
It has been permitted to
be proprietarily owned.

01:01:14.930 --> 01:01:19.500
How would should antitrust
law address this powerful

01:01:19.500 --> 01:01:22.540
inherent feature of an
information economy?

01:01:22.540 --> 01:01:26.900
I'll also add to this, that
some of these companies might be

01:01:26.900 --> 01:01:30.170
because of network issues,
natural monopolies,

01:01:30.170 --> 01:01:35.070
rather than the kinds
of industries that are,

01:01:35.070 --> 01:01:37.530
you have like a classical competition,

01:01:37.530 --> 01:01:39.490
of a bunch of small firms.

01:01:39.490 --> 01:01:41.660
How does that affect your perspective

01:01:41.660 --> 01:01:44.920
toward regulation in these markets?

01:01:44.920 --> 01:01:48.770
- Yeah, there are ways to
deal with this problem.

01:01:48.770 --> 01:01:53.220
The first is to change
laws and regulations,

01:01:53.220 --> 01:01:56.130
to affect the property rights of the data.

01:01:56.130 --> 01:01:59.340
Right now, it's really not
clear what belongs to me

01:01:59.340 --> 01:02:01.560
in terms of my data, what I can control,

01:02:01.560 --> 01:02:03.180
how I can control it,

01:02:03.180 --> 01:02:04.950
the same with a business.

01:02:04.950 --> 01:02:07.670
If that business finds
me through a platform,

01:02:07.670 --> 01:02:10.560
does that business have a right
to know something about me

01:02:10.560 --> 01:02:12.650
or can the platform keep
it all and withhold it

01:02:12.650 --> 01:02:14.240
from the business?

01:02:14.240 --> 01:02:18.670
To how much do I share and
does the business have a right

01:02:18.670 --> 01:02:21.980
to sell onto others or
to use it internally,

01:02:21.980 --> 01:02:25.760
in other totally unrelated activities,

01:02:25.760 --> 01:02:28.400
that happened to be under
the same corporate umbrella.

01:02:28.400 --> 01:02:30.620
So, there's a whole set
of what I might call

01:02:30.620 --> 01:02:33.330
consumer protection kinds
of issues around data,

01:02:33.330 --> 01:02:35.850
that would be very helpful
if we had more laws

01:02:35.850 --> 01:02:37.000
and regulations around.

01:02:38.080 --> 01:02:39.980
The second issue of the
network effects issue,

01:02:39.980 --> 01:02:43.640
comes back to interoperability
which I mentioned earlier,

01:02:43.640 --> 01:02:48.110
the internet is a giant
network of email traffic,

01:02:48.110 --> 01:02:50.850
where all of us have an ISP that had,

01:02:50.850 --> 01:02:54.110
that where that's our home,
and we can write to each other.

01:02:54.110 --> 01:02:56.710
And we can write to
anybody who has an eye,

01:02:56.710 --> 01:02:59.530
who's on an ISP that conforms
to the internet protocol.

01:03:00.490 --> 01:03:03.090
That is not true, if we
think about Facebook,

01:03:03.090 --> 01:03:05.110
that is not true, if we think about,

01:03:05.110 --> 01:03:07.710
proprietary social networks.

01:03:07.710 --> 01:03:10.420
There's no reason why we as a society,

01:03:10.420 --> 01:03:13.220
can't decide that social
networks should be interoperable.

01:03:13.220 --> 01:03:15.410
Just the same way email
and telephones are,

01:03:15.410 --> 01:03:18.650
I have an AT and T phone, it
can call your Verizon phone.

01:03:18.650 --> 01:03:21.790
Why is that? Because it's a rule.

01:03:21.790 --> 01:03:23.450
And we could have those same rules,

01:03:23.450 --> 01:03:25.570
for other businesses also.

01:03:25.570 --> 01:03:27.750
And once you have that rule,

01:03:27.750 --> 01:03:31.730
then it doesn't matter how
big your social network is,

01:03:31.730 --> 01:03:34.580
because the users of it can communicate

01:03:34.580 --> 01:03:36.580
with everybody else on
other social networks.

01:03:36.580 --> 01:03:39.540
Just like I can have a very tiny ISP,

01:03:39.540 --> 01:03:41.990
and it's on the internet
communicating with,

01:03:41.990 --> 01:03:43.710
emailing everybody else.

01:03:43.710 --> 01:03:46.170
So, this is a very simple idea,

01:03:46.170 --> 01:03:50.070
we've used it before, and it
gets rid of entry barriers,

01:03:50.070 --> 01:03:51.820
that are driven by network effects.

01:03:53.930 --> 01:03:56.240
- Thanks, I've actually
never heard that idea before.

01:03:56.240 --> 01:03:58.367
That's actually a really good idea.

01:03:58.367 --> 01:03:59.772
- Thank you.

01:03:59.772 --> 01:04:02.340
- Thank you, Fiona.

01:04:02.340 --> 01:04:05.370
Jacob, so, moving back around
the panel in reverse order,

01:04:05.370 --> 01:04:08.010
Jacob you mentioned cost control measures,

01:04:08.010 --> 01:04:10.470
a question it was asked,

01:04:10.470 --> 01:04:15.470
what's your favorite cost
control measure for medical care?

01:04:16.410 --> 01:04:19.000
- Well, I like them all to be honest.

01:04:19.000 --> 01:04:20.940
I mean, I really don't think,

01:04:20.940 --> 01:04:24.330
I really don't to use the cliche, right?

01:04:24.330 --> 01:04:26.070
I'm a belt and suspenders kind of guy,

01:04:26.070 --> 01:04:27.160
when it comes to cost control,

01:04:27.160 --> 01:04:29.310
we're have to do a lot
of different things.

01:04:30.180 --> 01:04:34.030
Let me mention three, one
which is very conventional,

01:04:34.030 --> 01:04:35.720
one that's kind of a
little more out there,

01:04:35.720 --> 01:04:39.730
and I think links up with
some of what Fiona said,

01:04:39.730 --> 01:04:43.700
and a third that's I'll keep you guessing,

01:04:43.700 --> 01:04:44.880
and I'll do this quickly.

01:04:44.880 --> 01:04:49.210
So, the first is like
when prices are too high,

01:04:49.210 --> 01:04:52.860
is not a bad idea in the
context of distorted markets

01:04:52.860 --> 01:04:55.200
like healthcare to try to control prices.

01:04:55.200 --> 01:04:58.980
So, that, I mean, that has
been a really key part,

01:04:58.980 --> 01:05:01.810
of how other countries have
dealt with medical costs,

01:05:01.810 --> 01:05:05.570
and it should be a key
part of how we do it.

01:05:05.570 --> 01:05:08.280
And it doesn't have to be that
you're dictating the price,

01:05:08.280 --> 01:05:11.130
you could start by just
simply having a level

01:05:11.130 --> 01:05:12.980
above which prices cannot go.

01:05:12.980 --> 01:05:14.960
I mean, one of the nice
features of the legislation,

01:05:14.960 --> 01:05:17.270
that Fiona helped with
on surprise medical bills

01:05:17.270 --> 01:05:18.450
was simply just saying,

01:05:19.720 --> 01:05:22.430
we're not gonna say that
it has to be the price X,

01:05:22.430 --> 01:05:24.020
but it cannot be higher than Y.

01:05:25.350 --> 01:05:27.840
And because people don't have any ability

01:05:27.840 --> 01:05:29.990
to decide in advance that
they're gonna get these

01:05:29.990 --> 01:05:32.180
surprise medical bills
and therefore to choose

01:05:32.180 --> 01:05:33.630
what the prices will be.

01:05:33.630 --> 01:05:35.300
So, I've often thought that,

01:05:35.300 --> 01:05:37.230
in addition to a public option,

01:05:37.230 --> 01:05:42.140
we should have basically
a price ceiling outside

01:05:42.140 --> 01:05:43.920
of the public option,

01:05:43.920 --> 01:05:46.210
that is based on the public option prices

01:05:46.210 --> 01:05:49.620
but is a multiple of them
and is moving downwards.

01:05:49.620 --> 01:05:53.460
That's, so, price, I'm not
saying that we should have

01:05:53.460 --> 01:05:55.140
fee for service medicine forever,

01:05:56.082 --> 01:05:58.500
but we have a lot of fee
for service medicine,

01:05:58.500 --> 01:06:00.590
we should put caps on prices.

01:06:00.590 --> 01:06:02.790
It's what every other country does.

01:06:02.790 --> 01:06:06.780
So, the second thing is
actually relates to the,

01:06:06.780 --> 01:06:08.450
to something that Fiona was alluding to,

01:06:08.450 --> 01:06:11.550
which is the enormous
amount of public investment,

01:06:11.550 --> 01:06:15.250
that goes into and public
support that goes into,

01:06:15.250 --> 01:06:20.250
a lot of the basic medical
goods that we enjoy, right?

01:06:20.760 --> 01:06:23.600
So, pharmaceuticals are a key example.

01:06:23.600 --> 01:06:25.960
So, in addition to like
thinking about antitrust,

01:06:25.960 --> 01:06:28.550
I think the flip side of that
is thinking about patents,

01:06:28.550 --> 01:06:31.200
and about intellectual
property in a different way.

01:06:31.200 --> 01:06:34.330
And the vaccine development
process I think,

01:06:34.330 --> 01:06:35.780
that we just went through really

01:06:35.780 --> 01:06:37.740
highlights the issue here, right?

01:06:37.740 --> 01:06:40.970
If it absolutely makes sense,
we should be pouring billions

01:06:40.970 --> 01:06:43.100
and billions and billions,
hundreds of billions of dollars,

01:06:43.100 --> 01:06:45.220
in developing a vaccine for COVID-19.

01:06:45.220 --> 01:06:46.850
It doesn't make a whole lot of sense,

01:06:46.850 --> 01:06:49.390
that then the pricing should
be entirely determined

01:06:49.390 --> 01:06:50.280
by private companies,

01:06:50.280 --> 01:06:52.890
who in this context may behave
in a very responsible way,

01:06:52.890 --> 01:06:55.860
but in general, the system
is really screwed up.

01:06:55.860 --> 01:06:57.760
So, that is a big deal,

01:06:57.760 --> 01:06:59.420
and it's going to be a
bigger deal going forward

01:06:59.420 --> 01:07:01.950
as biotechnological innovation.

01:07:01.950 --> 01:07:02.970
So, I put that there.

01:07:02.970 --> 01:07:07.970
So, the really the last kind
of idea out there that I think,

01:07:08.060 --> 01:07:12.380
is really kind of Goffian
given what we've said,

01:07:12.380 --> 01:07:15.470
is that I used to scoff a
little bit of this idea that,

01:07:15.470 --> 01:07:17.460
oh, we should be really
thinking a lot about,

01:07:17.460 --> 01:07:19.840
all the other things
that affect health care.

01:07:19.840 --> 01:07:23.250
You just can't see what's
happening before us right now,

01:07:23.250 --> 01:07:24.770
or the changes in health
that are occurring

01:07:24.770 --> 01:07:27.270
among key populations without recognizing,

01:07:27.270 --> 01:07:29.930
that we're spending in
really bad ways often.

01:07:29.930 --> 01:07:32.430
We're paying it for a
really expensive acute care

01:07:32.430 --> 01:07:33.940
especially at end of life,

01:07:33.940 --> 01:07:35.980
and we should be investing a lot more.

01:07:35.980 --> 01:07:38.510
And just like when we're
thinking about policing,

01:07:38.510 --> 01:07:40.370
well, should we be trying to deal

01:07:40.370 --> 01:07:43.800
with these problems at the back end?

01:07:43.800 --> 01:07:45.910
Or should we be doing more
investment on the front end?

01:07:45.910 --> 01:07:48.610
Or should we be thinking about
also alternative modalities

01:07:48.610 --> 01:07:50.370
for dealing with the
problems at the back end,

01:07:50.370 --> 01:07:51.740
should be dealing with mental health,

01:07:51.740 --> 01:07:55.290
as opposed to with
criminal criminal justice?

01:07:55.290 --> 01:07:56.300
I feel like in healthcare,

01:07:56.300 --> 01:07:58.770
we haven't done nearly enough of that.

01:07:58.770 --> 01:08:01.870
Well, it's a lot of
times paid lip service,

01:08:01.870 --> 01:08:04.840
and a lot of the stuff that we
pay lip service to is stupid.

01:08:04.840 --> 01:08:07.910
Like, we need more wellness
programs in the workplace.

01:08:07.910 --> 01:08:10.870
Like, that's fine, but
it's not the big thing.

01:08:10.870 --> 01:08:14.470
The big thing is there a huge
pockets of American society,

01:08:14.470 --> 01:08:18.770
whose health suffering, because
we've seen rampant racism,

01:08:18.770 --> 01:08:21.460
or an opioid epidemic that's
due to the destruction

01:08:21.460 --> 01:08:26.460
of jobs and opportunity and
key parts of the country.

01:08:27.490 --> 01:08:29.780
And we're not gonna deal
with their healthcare prices,

01:08:29.780 --> 01:08:32.280
if we don't start to
think much more about,

01:08:32.280 --> 01:08:35.130
what is the pipeline that
is producing the problems,

01:08:35.130 --> 01:08:36.840
that we are dealing with down the road.

01:08:36.840 --> 01:08:39.500
- Okay, Jacob let me pick up on that.

01:08:39.500 --> 01:08:41.290
- Yeah, thanks a lot.

01:08:41.290 --> 01:08:43.720
- On the third point and turn to Phil,

01:08:43.720 --> 01:08:47.540
and just say Phil and Jacob is,

01:08:47.540 --> 01:08:50.360
was alluding to some of the root causes,

01:08:50.360 --> 01:08:53.830
in your description of
the policing issues.

01:08:53.830 --> 01:08:57.160
You were also talking
about social determinants,

01:08:57.160 --> 01:08:59.570
and I wanted to know if you have anything

01:08:59.570 --> 01:09:03.700
you'd like to share on
how the educational system

01:09:03.700 --> 01:09:06.010
contributes to this or could,

01:09:06.010 --> 01:09:09.160
do you have any ideas for,

01:09:09.160 --> 01:09:11.260
how the educational system could be,

01:09:12.610 --> 01:09:15.000
used to help address
some of these problems?

01:09:15.000 --> 01:09:17.950
Do you have any views about the quality

01:09:17.950 --> 01:09:22.620
of the educational system
that people experience?

01:09:22.620 --> 01:09:25.100
- Yeah, I mean, so, one of
the ways that I've found

01:09:25.100 --> 01:09:27.930
to be newly annoying of late,

01:09:27.930 --> 01:09:31.730
is to ask the question back
to everyone, which one?

01:09:31.730 --> 01:09:33.410
So, when you say the
education system, which one?

01:09:33.410 --> 01:09:35.040
Are we talking about higher education?

01:09:35.040 --> 01:09:38.760
My hope is that I am currently
warping the minds of,

01:09:38.760 --> 01:09:41.020
150 some undergraduates, graduate,

01:09:41.020 --> 01:09:42.410
and professional school students,

01:09:42.410 --> 01:09:45.240
to get them to think about some
of these issues differently.

01:09:45.240 --> 01:09:46.550
I imagine that you were talking about

01:09:46.550 --> 01:09:47.800
the primary education system.

01:09:47.800 --> 01:09:51.280
Don't worry, I'm gonna answer it tomorrow.

01:09:51.280 --> 01:09:53.630
In which is, I said, okay then which one?

01:09:53.630 --> 01:09:56.340
Because in several of
the cities where we work,

01:09:56.340 --> 01:09:59.670
because of the reaction
against integration,

01:09:59.670 --> 01:10:04.670
they have private schools
that are so dirt cheap,

01:10:04.670 --> 01:10:06.230
in majority white neighborhoods,

01:10:06.230 --> 01:10:08.630
that they functionally have
reproduced segregation,

01:10:08.630 --> 01:10:10.680
which is why Nikole Hannah Jones finds,

01:10:10.680 --> 01:10:13.070
essentially, we've got
schools that are just everest,

01:10:13.070 --> 01:10:14.700
not more segregated than they were

01:10:14.700 --> 01:10:17.020
before brown vs board of education.

01:10:17.020 --> 01:10:18.980
Good job, all deliberate speed.

01:10:19.900 --> 01:10:21.670
So, I think we have to
think about what we mean,

01:10:21.670 --> 01:10:24.720
by education and what
role we want it to play.

01:10:24.720 --> 01:10:27.260
That said, I've been shocked.

01:10:27.260 --> 01:10:29.740
I mean, Alan, I've been working with,

01:10:29.740 --> 01:10:31.820
increasingly complex models,

01:10:31.820 --> 01:10:34.520
for how we predict racial disparities,

01:10:34.520 --> 01:10:37.010
and where the variants
lives in those ratios.

01:10:37.010 --> 01:10:40.580
I'm shocked by how much primary education,

01:10:40.580 --> 01:10:41.940
doesn't soak up variants.

01:10:41.940 --> 01:10:43.930
Once you start looking at just basically,

01:10:43.930 --> 01:10:47.810
race, poverty and and sort of crime

01:10:47.810 --> 01:10:49.410
as if it's not already baked in.

01:10:50.390 --> 01:10:53.190
But there's good research that suggests,

01:10:53.190 --> 01:10:55.720
the longer kids are in school
literally during the day,

01:10:55.720 --> 01:10:56.970
and the more time they're in school

01:10:56.970 --> 01:10:58.170
over the course of the summer,

01:10:58.170 --> 01:11:00.250
the less likely they are to get involved

01:11:00.250 --> 01:11:02.200
in petty crimes, right?

01:11:02.200 --> 01:11:04.940
So, you could make an argument that,

01:11:04.940 --> 01:11:07.580
if we made schools better caretakers

01:11:07.580 --> 01:11:10.440
of particularly adolescent children,

01:11:10.440 --> 01:11:15.310
that we could put a
debt in the crime rates.

01:11:15.310 --> 01:11:18.240
I'd also though go back to this and say,

01:11:18.240 --> 01:11:22.190
if the way that we deal
with adolescents acting out,

01:11:22.190 --> 01:11:25.730
is punishing them with
handcuffs and cages,

01:11:25.730 --> 01:11:28.450
maybe the problem wasn't
the kids in the first place,

01:11:28.450 --> 01:11:30.540
but that we imagined that something about

01:11:30.540 --> 01:11:33.200
normal adolescent
development was criminal.

01:11:33.200 --> 01:11:36.510
So, there are absolutely
ways that it seems like,

01:11:36.510 --> 01:11:39.590
increasing coverage and resources,

01:11:39.590 --> 01:11:42.280
for schools can make a debt,

01:11:42.280 --> 01:11:44.640
particularly adolescent crime rates.

01:11:44.640 --> 01:11:46.690
But that's only within
context where we've taken

01:11:46.690 --> 01:11:49.080
for granted, that it's
okay to criminalize,

01:11:49.080 --> 01:11:50.570
what absolutely is a criminal

01:11:50.570 --> 01:11:52.030
and more affluent neighborhoods.

01:11:52.030 --> 01:11:54.570
Or at least it seems that way.

01:11:54.570 --> 01:11:55.890
The trade-off between the two,

01:11:55.890 --> 01:11:57.550
like we have more control,

01:11:57.550 --> 01:12:00.730
over public education in
vulnerable communities.

01:12:00.730 --> 01:12:02.980
So, more resources and
more counseling there,

01:12:02.980 --> 01:12:05.840
I mean, we have literally
tens of thousands

01:12:05.840 --> 01:12:08.250
of jurisdictions that have no counselors,

01:12:08.250 --> 01:12:11.240
but hundreds of law
enforcement in the schools.

01:12:11.240 --> 01:12:13.980
So, for sure more resources,
we could make a debt.

01:12:13.980 --> 01:12:16.120
And I'm in favor of doing that,

01:12:16.120 --> 01:12:18.620
of creating the social
services and expanded hours,

01:12:18.620 --> 01:12:21.120
and capacity for primary education

01:12:21.120 --> 01:12:22.720
in vulnerable communities.

01:12:22.720 --> 01:12:25.720
But the longterm solve of this I think,

01:12:25.720 --> 01:12:30.720
is solving the problem of
what is, and isn't punishable,

01:12:31.620 --> 01:12:33.310
were punishment worthy,

01:12:33.310 --> 01:12:36.180
in the communities where we've
given them fewer options.

01:12:36.180 --> 01:12:38.880
And that I think is
likely not gonna be a job,

01:12:38.880 --> 01:12:40.390
that primary education can do.

01:12:40.390 --> 01:12:42.870
Hopefully, it's a job
that folks who graduate,

01:12:42.870 --> 01:12:44.390
from secondary education will do

01:12:44.390 --> 01:12:46.470
as they move into positions of power.

01:12:46.470 --> 01:12:48.100
- Great, Phil, thank you so much.

