I've had a lot of fights,

01:17:32.080 --> 01:17:35.080
with the right over the public option,

01:17:35.080 --> 01:17:38.210
and I don't find myself
particularly convinced.

01:17:38.210 --> 01:17:42.430
And so, and I feel like a
lot, like it's very revealing,

01:17:42.430 --> 01:17:46.660
that the people who remain on
the hard right on healthcare

01:17:46.660 --> 01:17:49.730
who are the ones who help
design say the repeal

01:17:49.730 --> 01:17:54.500
and replace legislation
that didn't happen in 2017,

01:17:54.500 --> 01:17:56.420
did they become increasingly removed,

01:17:56.420 --> 01:17:59.210
not just from the ideas I support,

01:17:59.210 --> 01:18:01.010
but from the ideas they want supported.

01:18:01.010 --> 01:18:05.280
Like the mandatory private
health insurance model.

01:18:05.280 --> 01:18:08.230
So, like Stuart, I'm a
big fan of Stuart Butler,

01:18:08.230 --> 01:18:09.550
who used to be at the Heritage Foundation,

01:18:09.550 --> 01:18:10.730
now he's at the Brookings Institution,

01:18:10.730 --> 01:18:12.720
so, I can't say that
these are conservative.

01:18:12.720 --> 01:18:14.650
But on that side, on the left,

01:18:14.650 --> 01:18:17.390
I mean they come after me,
right, no pun intended,

01:18:17.390 --> 01:18:18.290
right and left.

01:18:18.290 --> 01:18:19.280
And on the left,

01:18:19.280 --> 01:18:23.490
I really liked Jerry Freeman
at Amherst, UMass Amherst.

01:18:23.490 --> 01:18:26.010
She's been writing a lot
about Medicare for all lately.

01:18:26.010 --> 01:18:27.210
Don't always agree with them,

01:18:27.210 --> 01:18:29.620
but he worked a lot with Bernie Sanders,

01:18:29.620 --> 01:18:31.830
and he has some interesting
recent writings.

01:18:31.830 --> 01:18:35.060
He has a new book, which
I had near me lately,

01:18:35.060 --> 01:18:37.830
which is making the case
for Medicare for all.

01:18:37.830 --> 01:18:39.340
- Terrific, thank you very much, Jacob.

01:18:39.340 --> 01:18:41.770
And again, thank you
for all of your comments

01:18:41.770 --> 01:18:43.950
and participation in tonight's panel.

01:18:43.950 --> 01:18:48.490
And now, to wrap up, I
guess, a Fiona Scott Morton.

01:18:48.490 --> 01:18:50.940
- I just wanna second what Jacob said.

01:18:50.940 --> 01:18:53.140
I will say that the reason that repeal

01:18:53.140 --> 01:18:55.130
and replace never got a replace,

01:18:55.130 --> 01:18:57.580
is because the Affordable
Care Act was a comp

01:18:57.580 --> 01:18:59.970
was supposed to be, was
a Republican proposal.

01:18:59.970 --> 01:19:00.850
It's Romneycare.

01:19:00.850 --> 01:19:04.450
It's outsourcing government
provided health insurance,

01:19:04.450 --> 01:19:06.040
to the private sector.

01:19:06.040 --> 01:19:08.050
So, if you want, if you're
not gonna support that,

01:19:08.050 --> 01:19:10.020
you're left with just letting people die,

01:19:10.020 --> 01:19:13.760
which has been the default
alternative on the right.

01:19:13.760 --> 01:19:15.470
So, there really isn't
anybody to argue with.

01:19:15.470 --> 01:19:17.040
It's kind of like it's either,

01:19:17.040 --> 01:19:19.330
the Affordable Care Act
or move left from that.

01:19:19.330 --> 01:19:22.110
So, that's a problem for the
intellectual debate there.

01:19:23.110 --> 01:19:25.910
So, what do I follow
that I think is great.

01:19:25.910 --> 01:19:28.010
There's a blog run by
the Washington Center

01:19:28.010 --> 01:19:30.910
for Equitable Growth
called Competitive Edge,

01:19:30.910 --> 01:19:33.850
which has really nice anti-trust analysis

01:19:33.850 --> 01:19:36.100
and competition topics.

01:19:36.100 --> 01:19:39.430
Slightly broader covering
more regulatory topics,

01:19:39.430 --> 01:19:42.030
as well as anti-trust is the blog

01:19:42.030 --> 01:19:43.410
at the University of Chicago,

01:19:43.410 --> 01:19:45.650
Booth School of Business
called Pro Market,

01:19:45.650 --> 01:19:47.350
run by Luigi Zingales.

01:19:47.350 --> 01:19:51.550
And that's a great source
of really fun topics,

01:19:51.550 --> 01:19:54.510
and both easy to read and very accessible.

01:19:57.150 --> 01:19:59.580
Yeah, I have to answer your
second question for me.

01:20:01.240 --> 01:20:03.660
In my field the other side to the people

01:20:03.660 --> 01:20:08.110
who want competition are the
people want corporate profits.

01:20:08.110 --> 01:20:10.740
If you do the economic analysis, right,

01:20:10.740 --> 01:20:12.650
you maximize social
welfare with competition.

01:20:12.650 --> 01:20:15.830
So, there isn't an economist
who doesn't think that's true.

01:20:16.860 --> 01:20:18.780
And so, the other side of
this debate are the folks

01:20:18.780 --> 01:20:20.650
who want corporate profits there,

01:20:22.160 --> 01:20:23.950
corporations obviously,

01:20:23.950 --> 01:20:27.090
the people they pay to do scholarship,

01:20:27.090 --> 01:20:29.790
or that they encouraged to do scholarship,

01:20:29.790 --> 01:20:33.600
for reasons that are too long
to explain on this event,

01:20:33.600 --> 01:20:37.020
kind of stopped using economics tools

01:20:37.020 --> 01:20:38.030
around 1980.

01:20:38.030 --> 01:20:40.610
Or that is to say the tools
they use go up to 1980,

01:20:40.610 --> 01:20:44.450
but they don't use economics
after 1980 more or less.

01:20:44.450 --> 01:20:47.370
And so, that means there's a real shortage

01:20:47.370 --> 01:20:50.580
of academics and scholars in my field,

01:20:50.580 --> 01:20:54.040
with whom you could debate
because the people who are

01:20:54.040 --> 01:20:57.590
in favor of profits, aren't
really in the journals.

01:20:57.590 --> 01:20:59.160
So, I'm sorry,

01:20:59.160 --> 01:21:01.780
that's a kind of an incomplete
answer, but there it is.

01:21:01.780 --> 01:21:03.060
- All right, there you go.

01:21:03.060 --> 01:21:05.490
Thank you Fiona again, thank you for that,

01:21:05.490 --> 01:21:09.670
and thank you for your
participation in tonight's panel.

